WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:02.250
When it comes to evaluating

2
00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:05.280
the greatness of elite performers,

3
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.910
we overemphasize peak performance

4
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.370
and underemphasize longevity.

5
00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:16.020
And that is driving a lot of young people out of the sport

6
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.090
and discouraging a lot of people

7
00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.220
and frustrating our attempt

8
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.562
to restore the health of our society.

9
00:00:23.562 --> 00:00:25.290 line:15%
(groovy music)

10
00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.870 line:15%
1% of the population in middle age

11
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.550 line:15%
is practicing healthy habits.

12
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.383
Why is that?

13
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:32.340
'Cause no one bothered to teach them

14
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.190
at the moment when those habits need to be taught.

15
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:39.180
The idea of having a standard of performance

16
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.603
that spans 20 years, that mattered.

17
00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.330
Hey, everybody, welcome to the podcast.

18
00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.610
All is right and well in the world

19
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.093
because my guest today is Malcolm Gladwell.

20
00:00:52.003 --> 00:00:52.836
(imitates head exploding)

21
00:00:52.836 --> 00:00:54.330
It's so good.

22
00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.900
It's one that goes in so many different directions,

23
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.930
as you might suspect, because well, it is Malcolm.

24
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.430
It's a fascinating discussion,

25
00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.680
everything that I hoped it would be.

26
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.520
I suspect many of you are already familiar with Malcolm.

27
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.000
Let's face it, he's one of our most prolific authors.

28
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.287
Perhaps you've listened to his sensational podcast,

29
00:01:14.287 --> 00:01:15.360
"Revisionist History,"

30
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.390
or read one of his many "New York Times" bestselling books

31
00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.737
like "Tipping Point," "Blank," "Outliers,"

32
00:01:20.737 --> 00:01:23.730
"David and Goliath," and "Talking to Strangers."

33
00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:24.563
What you might not know

34
00:01:24.563 --> 00:01:27.240
is Malcolm is also president and co-founder

35
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.200
of Pushkin Industries, which is a true innovator in audio,

36
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.970
pushing new creative limits

37
00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.580
in the podcast and audiobook space.

38
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.310
He also happens to be a really great runner

39
00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.890
as well as a track and field aficionado.

40
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.860
And these are passions that he now is putting on display

41
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.300
in his new limited series podcast, "Legacy of Speed."

42
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.640
It's a series that tells this incredible story

43
00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.730
behind the San Jose State track and field team

44
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:57.630
that made history at the 1968 Summer Olympics,

45
00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.920
in both medals and in terms of athlete protests.

46
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.250
Which ended up paving the way for the modern-day activism

47
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.680
of our contemporary sports heroes.

48
00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.050
Today, we talk about all of that, of course,

49
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.930
as well as running and sport more broadly.

50
00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.810
Malcolm also shares ideas around education,

51
00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:19.260
around publishing, the future of audio, creativity,

52
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.030
and just tons more.

53
00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.040
It's a super-fun conversation.

54
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.290
Malcolm is somebody I've wanted to have on the show

55
00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:26.850
since day one.

56
00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.110
It finally happened.

57
00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.950 line:15%
I did my best to not fan out too much,

58
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.600 line:15%
and I hope you enjoy it.

59
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.710 line:15%
So please hit that subscribe button, and here we go.

60
00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.440
All right, man, nice to finally meet you .

61
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.150
Been looking forward to this for a long time.

62
00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.530
Oh good, me too.

63
00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:47.913
I was reflecting on the many things

64
00:02:47.913 --> 00:02:49.890
that I wanna get into with you today.

65
00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:51.690
But the first thing I gotta clear up

66
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.563
has to do with our mutual friend David Epstein.

67
00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.260
Who has gone on the record

68
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.129
to say that you are the world's ultimate sandbagger.

69
00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:03.570 line:15%
(both laughing)

70
00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.823 line:15%
Now, that's, first of all,

71
00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.510
I will only say that there is precious little upside

72
00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:17.460
to overstating your expectations in any,

73
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:20.957
so better to underpromise and overdeliver.

74
00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.600
In all areas of life, not just in writing.

75
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.160
To have people underestimate you

76
00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.240
is a sort of underestimated superpower.

77
00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.170
Yes.

78
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:35.820
Also in running, I do that because I'm typically the oldest.

79
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.040
In any group I go running with,

80
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.130
I'm usually the oldest by like 20 years,

81
00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.720
sometimes I'm twice.

82
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.350
When I used to run with this track club in New York,

83
00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.000
and I would literally be twice the age

84
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.333
of the people that I was training with.

85
00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:54.333
So in that situation, you don't wanna go out boasting,

86
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.550
you have to make it very plain that you're the old man.

87
00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.430
Is that the track that's down on the lower east side?

88
00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.263
Yeah.

89
00:04:00.263 --> 00:04:01.660
And then I would run in Brooklyn as well.

90
00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:03.810
There, there were people who were like,

91
00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.493
I was almost three times as old.

92
00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.303
So you already have the age thing built in

93
00:04:10.303 --> 00:04:13.560
as a factor or a reason for people to underestimate you.

94
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.583
But then you go the extra mile to double down on that.

95
00:04:18.912 --> 00:04:19.745
There's no...

96
00:04:19.745 --> 00:04:21.210
(both laughing)

97
00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.430
How does that play into the other areas of your life,

98
00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.320
like when you're approaching people to speak to

99
00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:30.320
or in the terrain of what you do for a living?

100
00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.293
I'm Canadian, so this is a national trait.

101
00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.330
We're a tiny country that is in the shadow

102
00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.360
of it's large Bella Coast neighbor.

103
00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.650
And we like to stay humble

104
00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:51.150
Yes, it is not becoming to be boastful.

105
00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:52.440
We're quiet and apologetic,

106
00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:56.640
and I think those are important traits to maintain

107
00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.050
I agree.

108
00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:59.430
We saw this play out

109
00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:02.730
in the infamous influencer mile the other year

110
00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:06.450
with you and our other mutual friend, Chris Chavez.

111
00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.433
Which turned into a blowout.

112
00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:11.913
I loved watching that, that was super-fun.

113
00:05:12.838 --> 00:05:16.830
I think it dovetails into a terrain

114
00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.873
that I wanna explore with you.

115
00:05:19.892 --> 00:05:23.100
Which is, how can we make sports,

116
00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.043
these off kilter sports that we love,

117
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.213
more interesting to mainstream audiences?

118
00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:35.850
I love running, I'm more in the ultra trail running universe

119
00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.610
I'm less of a track and field aficionado.

120
00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.890
I'm not the guy who's tuning in

121
00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:44.550
to watch the livestream of whatever meet, unlike you.

122
00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:49.550
But I was sure to be online when that was going down

123
00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.320
'cause I had to see what was gonna happen (laughs).

124
00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:53.730
Well, you know Chris.

125
00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:54.870
Yeah.

126
00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:57.030
So in answer your question,

127
00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:59.250
part one is we need more people like Chris Chavez.

128
00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:02.580
So those listening, Chris is a just a young man

129
00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:07.580
who has a lot of imagination and creativity

130
00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.560
and who's chosen to put that to work

131
00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:12.900
to promote a sport he loves, track and field.

132
00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:17.760
And you realize every sport that's taken off

133
00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.890
has had a moment when it's...

134
00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.950
Look at what happened with F1 after the Netflix-

135
00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.560
Oh, I'm a a product of that, I'm obsessed now.

136
00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.420
And I previously found that sport impenetrable

137
00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:32.850
So a group of people with imagination

138
00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.980
chose to tell a story around a sport

139
00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:37.560
that opened it up to all kinds of...

140
00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.023
And you could do the same thing with,

141
00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:44.340
you can look at sports that have taken off

142
00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.710
and you see some moment where there is a compelling story

143
00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.510
that people were choosing.

144
00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:52.080
Billy Jean King and women's tennis, to go way way back.

145
00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.080
Or in the '70s the NBA was worse than irrelevant,

146
00:06:58.590 --> 00:06:59.520
it was an after...

147
00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.440
Teams were selling for nothing,

148
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.380
players, they could walk down the street

149
00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.330
and no one would know who they are,

150
00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.100
there's all of these.

151
00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.390
We do need this kind of infusion of that's part one.

152
00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:14.160
But part two I would say is,

153
00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.560
and this is a bigger problem with running in particular,

154
00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:21.560
is we're not growing the base aggressively enough.

155
00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:28.080
I had this idea that I was talking to someone about recently

156
00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.510
which is, think about something

157
00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:33.303
as simple as high school cross country.

158
00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:38.040
The way high school cross country is scored

159
00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:42.030
is you'll have a team, maybe it's five people,

160
00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.220
and you'll combine,

161
00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.950
you get X number of points for first place,

162
00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:50.310
so many points for second place, you combine the points.

163
00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:53.583
It's heavily weighted the team with the fastest runners,

164
00:07:54.605 --> 00:07:59.605
the team with the most elite runner tends to win right?

165
00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.560
Because you get extra.

166
00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.150
Why do we structure it that way?

167
00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.310
Why don't we say, first of all,

168
00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.380
in order to compete in a cross country meet,

169
00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:10.590
you must have a minimum of 20 people on your team.

170
00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:11.880
And then why don't we say

171
00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:15.360
scoring is simply the combined time

172
00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.190
of all 20 of your runners.

173
00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:19.050
Kind of GC and Tour de France.

174
00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:19.883
Yes.

175
00:08:19.883 --> 00:08:23.613
So your 20th runner matters as much as your first runner.

176
00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:26.970
Now, that changes the psychological makeup

177
00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:28.410
of the sport dramatically.

178
00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:32.400
Right now, if I'm the 20th best runner at a high school,

179
00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.950
what's my motivation for joining the cross country team?

180
00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:39.300
It's zero, I don't count, I don't even score in meets,

181
00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:41.970
I'm way, way behind the first person.

182
00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.970
But now under this system, the 20th person,

183
00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:48.330
we are as passionately interested in how well they run

184
00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:50.334
as we are in the first.

185
00:08:50.334 --> 00:08:52.950
And the the fact that we have to get away

186
00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:57.950
I think at earlier levels of sports at all levels,

187
00:08:58.806 --> 00:09:01.680
we can't impose adult models

188
00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.940
of hyper competitiveness on kids.

189
00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:09.900
Kids sports must look and be structured

190
00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.600
completely differently from elite sports.

191
00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:14.640
We cannot get away with this.

192
00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:17.160
We're indulging the fantasy life of parents

193
00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:19.230
who wanna believe that their 12-year-old

194
00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:23.130
is mimicking LeBron when they play basketball

195
00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:28.130
or the greatest American runners

196
00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:29.640
when they run cross country.

197
00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.640
No, we're playing a different game

198
00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.770
when you're 12 and 14 and 16.
Sure.

199
00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:36.990
So on some level, that's about

200
00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:41.990
injecting semi individual sports with a team component to it

201
00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:43.013
that creates-

202
00:09:43.013 --> 00:09:46.590
That is a mass team component to it.

203
00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:50.070
And then beyond that, it's shelving the idea

204
00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:52.620
of a winner takes all mindset.

205
00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:53.940
And really getting honest

206
00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:56.250
about the fact that high school sports

207
00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:57.810
or just kid's sports in general,

208
00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.480
for every LeBron, there's a million kids

209
00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.760
who are doing it for very different reasons

210
00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.320
and are getting value out of it

211
00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.010
in a way that has nothing to do with becoming a champion.

212
00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:09.330
And that's what we're missing

213
00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.551
I think in all of these sports.

214
00:10:11.551 --> 00:10:14.460
I've been doing team tries.

215
00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:15.560
What is that?

216
00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.890
Team triathlons where I'm the runner.

217
00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:20.723
Oh yeah, yeah, yeah.

218
00:10:20.723 --> 00:10:22.603
And we have a swimmer and we have a...

219
00:10:22.603 --> 00:10:26.310
I did that in Malibu with with Mary Kane and Alexi Pappas.

220
00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:27.283
Oh my goodness.

221
00:10:27.283 --> 00:10:28.414
Yeah, it was super-fun.

222
00:10:28.414 --> 00:10:33.414
(laughs) Well, and it was first of all, insanely fun.

223
00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:34.770
And I realized, oh it's a way...

224
00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:36.900
I'm never gonna do a triathlon, I can't swim

225
00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:38.730
and it's not gonna happen.

226
00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:40.500
I'll be the swimmer on your team Malcolm.

227
00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.188
Yeah, all right, all right, good.

228
00:10:42.188 --> 00:10:45.540
But I just thought, triathlon world should do,

229
00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.040
and when we do, we do these team tries

230
00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:49.110
and they'll be like, we'll go to these local triathlons

231
00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:52.890
and there'll be 200 competitors

232
00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:56.400
but only about three teams or four teams.

233
00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:57.960
And I think, what a lost opportunity?

234
00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.980
Because it's the team that makes the sport accessible.

235
00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.280
Lots of people can round up as a swimmer and a biker

236
00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.950
if they're a runner or some other combination.

237
00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:11.220
We should be putting the team triathlon front and center

238
00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.430
at the mass level, participation level,

239
00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:15.570
that opens up the sport.

240
00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:19.980
And also by the way, when we go to these little meets

241
00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:21.300
it is so much fun.

242
00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:24.960
I had no idea triathlons was such a wonderfully fun activity

243
00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:26.010
I'd never gone to one.

244
00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:26.843
Oh really?

245
00:11:26.843 --> 00:11:29.194
'Cause why would I have suspected,

246
00:11:29.194 --> 00:11:30.027
right?
Right, right, right.

247
00:11:30.027 --> 00:11:31.740
Well, yeah they are super-fun.

248
00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:35.070
Don't even get me started on how to reimagine triathlon.

249
00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:37.170
First of all, the swim is irrelevant

250
00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:38.550
in almost every triathlon.

251
00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.770
It's so short by comparison to the other legs.

252
00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:43.590
So it really favors the other sports

253
00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:45.690
and codules the non-swimmer,

254
00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:46.709
which drives me crazy as somebody

255
00:11:46.709 --> 00:11:49.270
who is first and foremost a swimmer.

256
00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:51.240
It was clearly invented by non-swimmers.

257
00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:54.040
They let you wear wetsuits when it's not not cold out.

258
00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.320
Even at Ironman level,

259
00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:01.110
you just have to stay in contact with everyone else.

260
00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:03.720
And then the other legs are so much more important.

261
00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:06.240
So that's absurd.

262
00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:08.250
And why don't we put the swim last

263
00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:09.840
if we really wanna make it interesting?

264
00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:10.710
And then beyond that,

265
00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:14.550
yes, relays are the thing that really makes it fun,

266
00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:15.540
makes it inclusive,

267
00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:18.090
and also is more interesting for the spectator,

268
00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:22.350
whether it's track and field or swimming or triathlon.

269
00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:26.670
Oh yeah, I think, like I said, it goes back,

270
00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:30.870
there's a lack of imagination in promoting the sport.

271
00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:34.830
And the people running sports are not thinking of promotion

272
00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:37.241
and broadening the base as their goal,

273
00:12:37.241 --> 00:12:39.650
they're servicing the needs of the elite athlete.

274
00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:41.880
And we have to understand that there are times

275
00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:44.190
when those two things are in conflict.

276
00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:48.687
And we have to decide well, who are we doing this for?

277
00:12:48.687 --> 00:12:51.330
And I would love a stretch where we decide,

278
00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:54.300
we're doing it for the ordinary athlete

279
00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:57.300
who wants to get out and have fun in this.

280
00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.000
These institutions are so calcified though,

281
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.700
it feels like a heavy lift.

282
00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:06.690
Have you ever had conversations with individuals

283
00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:09.930
who are organizers or at the heads of these leagues,

284
00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:13.923
et cetera to try to inject some of these ideas?

285
00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:17.700
I can only imagine.

286
00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:18.533
I know.

287
00:13:18.533 --> 00:13:22.110
So that's why the Billy Jean King thing and all of that,

288
00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:23.460
they're considered stunts.

289
00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:27.870
But actually they're catalysts I think for interests.

290
00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.730
And I know that a couple years ago,

291
00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:31.479
you challenged LeBron James to a mile.

292
00:13:31.479 --> 00:13:32.312
To a mile.

293
00:13:32.312 --> 00:13:33.145
That never happened.

294
00:13:33.145 --> 00:13:35.160
Did you ever get any response?

295
00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.050
No, so I didn't

296
00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.240
although I pressed it actually quite far.

297
00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:40.747
I did approach people at Nike and said,

298
00:13:40.747 --> 00:13:42.847
"Come on, just talk to LeBron about this."

299
00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:48.030
If I might explain my position this,

300
00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:50.280
the reason I wanted to do this

301
00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:53.700
is not that I thought I could beat LeBron, the opposite,

302
00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:55.080
I thought LeBron would beat me.

303
00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.730
So at the time I was probably good

304
00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:58.770
for around a five minute mile.

305
00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:02.133
I honestly believe that LeBron can go sub five.

306
00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.810
And I think it's because there's two things going on here,

307
00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:07.740
well, one thing.

308
00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:11.230
That basketball has been turned into an aerobic sport

309
00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:14.850
over the last generation.

310
00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:17.010
In the '70s, they were smoking cigarettes

311
00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:17.970
in the locker room.

312
00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.340
And when you watch a '70s basketball game,

313
00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:21.630
no one's playing any defense.

314
00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.210
It's defense, of course, where you exert all your effort.

315
00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:25.920
What they're doing is they're loafing

316
00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:27.570
until the ball's in their hands

317
00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.430
and then they explode to the hoop, right?

318
00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:31.890
Right.
That's an anaerobic sport.

319
00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:34.230
So you can smoke cigarettes in the locker room

320
00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:35.340
and you can be fine.

321
00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:40.340
Now, the players are exerting full effort the entire game,

322
00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.380
the entire time they're on the floor.

323
00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:45.750
They're two different sports.

324
00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:48.510
I watched a video of LeBron doing this exercise,

325
00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:53.100
where he was dribbling the length of the court dunking.

326
00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:54.240
And then without stopping,

327
00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.160
dribbling back to the other end, dunking.

328
00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.500
And he did this, it must have,

329
00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.050
I dunno how long it was, 10 minutes.

330
00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:03.060
And I was like, "Oh my God,

331
00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:04.827
this guy is an endurance athlete."

332
00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:09.900
And I thought LeBron can easily break five.

333
00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.930
I don't care that he's 6' 9", 250, he can break five.

334
00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:14.730
And the only way I'm gonna prove this

335
00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:19.730
is if a dedicated miler, such as myself, albeit super-old,

336
00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.730
takes on LeBron and LeBron cleans my clock.

337
00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:27.690
And then the world will realize, oh my God, this sport,

338
00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:30.540
basketball, is different than we thought.

339
00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:32.340
So that's the incentive for him to do it,

340
00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:34.410
but no chance in-

341
00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:35.797
If he loses needs to be however.

342
00:15:35.797 --> 00:15:36.630
(both laughing)

343
00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:37.563
Yeah, I guess so.

344
00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:40.740
Then I'm...

345
00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:41.573
I saw that video,

346
00:15:41.573 --> 00:15:45.210
I think it has like 124 million views on Instagram

347
00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:46.043
of LeBron just-

348
00:15:46.043 --> 00:15:47.644
Of LeBron dribbling.

349
00:15:47.644 --> 00:15:49.229
Yeah, it's impressive.
It's astonishing.

350
00:15:49.229 --> 00:15:53.373
It's like it's a steeple chase, that's what he's doing,

351
00:15:53.373 --> 00:15:54.720
he's jumping up.

352
00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:55.620
He's just looping,

353
00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:57.720
he's just going from one end of the court to the next.

354
00:15:57.720 --> 00:15:59.640
And he's got guys feeding him the ball

355
00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:00.649
for him to dunk at each end.

356
00:16:00.649 --> 00:16:03.480
It's incredible, it's an incredible workout, right?

357
00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:07.170
Well, the challenge that I'd like to see

358
00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:11.430
would be the literary mile between you and Nick Thompson.

359
00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:14.304
It could be like "The New Yorker" versus "The Atlantic."

360
00:16:14.304 --> 00:16:17.280
But you might have to do a 10K or something like that.

361
00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:18.840
He's a marathoner, you're-

362
00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:19.710
I've run with Nick.

363
00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:20.710
Oh, you do?

364
00:16:21.636 --> 00:16:22.650
I'm not even in his league.

365
00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:25.860
Nick is a two, what is he now, 228?

366
00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:26.730
Yeah, incredibly fast.

367
00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:28.960
Marathoner and is well into his 40s

368
00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:31.980
He's on a whole nother level.

369
00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:33.930
So you're not taking the bait on that one.

370
00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:35.080
No, I'm not, I'm not.

371
00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:39.570
If Nick's willing to give me some massive handicap,

372
00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:40.920
yes, I will.

373
00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:42.450
Can we age grade the results?

374
00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:43.950
This is the only chance I have

375
00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:46.205
Then maybe I'll...

376
00:16:46.205 --> 00:16:47.700
I don't know, man.

377
00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:49.140
I think it's about the distance.

378
00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:51.030
You have to pick something in the middle

379
00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:53.166
where you guys would measure up to each other really.

380
00:16:53.166 --> 00:16:55.110
I don't think I'm faster than him.

381
00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:56.430
We used to do loops.

382
00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:59.373
He once joined me and my little group of running friends.

383
00:17:00.361 --> 00:17:02.220
We were doing intervals

384
00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:05.880
around the Central Park reservoir in New York.

385
00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:09.453
And we were killing ourselves.

386
00:17:12.319 --> 00:17:14.427
He could have been on his phone the entire time.

387
00:17:14.427 --> 00:17:16.590
And that's how easy it was for him.

388
00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:19.053
So that was all a lesson for me.

389
00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:22.440
How does he maintain that fitness

390
00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:24.390
with the job that he has?

391
00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:25.223
I have no idea.

392
00:17:25.223 --> 00:17:26.160
Yeah, It's impressive.

393
00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:26.993
I have no idea.

394
00:17:26.993 --> 00:17:29.520
I feel like he doesn't get enough attention or due

395
00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:30.393
for his running.

396
00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:35.880
Well, he's not aging, he's getting better in his 40s.

397
00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:39.090
He ran his fastest marathon ever in his mid 40s

398
00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:41.040
That's kind of...

399
00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:44.580
I don't understand how he also stays healthy.

400
00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:46.780
That's of course the big issue with runners.

401
00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:49.350
I don't know how he does does that.

402
00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:54.300
I see him on Strava and he's running with a backpack on

403
00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:55.980
from Brooklyn to the office.

404
00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:57.493
That seems to be what he does.

405
00:17:57.493 --> 00:17:59.790
How do you think about that equation

406
00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:01.770
between aging and performance?

407
00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:03.990
Because we're seeing so many breakthroughs

408
00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:05.880
with athletes later in age.

409
00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:09.750
And as somebody who hosts a lot of ultra runners here,

410
00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:11.430
I had Camille Herron the other day,

411
00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:15.570
she's breaking world records at the 100 miles in her 40s,

412
00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:17.400
and beating all the guys and all that.

413
00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:20.883
Obviously the longer the distance, that gender gap narrows.

414
00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:24.570
But the performances that we're seeing

415
00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:27.990
continue to astound me in endurance sports

416
00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:29.040
and even in swimming.

417
00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.380
Remember there was that period when they had the speed suits

418
00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:33.180
and everybody thought well, those world records

419
00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:34.290
are never gonna get broken.

420
00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:37.530
And we just saw world championships in Budapest last week

421
00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:40.110
and all kinds of world records are continuing to get broken

422
00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:41.553
by really young people.

423
00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:46.080
How do you think about the upper limits of human performance

424
00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:48.870
and performance as we get older?

425
00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:51.595
Well, there's two, this is not news to you,

426
00:18:51.595 --> 00:18:55.930
but on the older part, it's clear that

427
00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:58.830
what we thought was a decline

428
00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:03.830
in physiological capacity as we age,

429
00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:06.670
may in a large part simply be

430
00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:09.630
an increased tendency to get it injured.

431
00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:12.240
I think the injury thing is huge here.

432
00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:17.240
That the last generation has seen this revolution

433
00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:19.770
in keeping older people healthy.

434
00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:21.390
And that when you extend the career

435
00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:24.310
of a whole group of athletes, you're gonna get

436
00:19:27.256 --> 00:19:29.670
the base of the pyramid is now just a lot broader,

437
00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:33.003
so we're obviously gonna see changes in the top.

438
00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:39.480
And the motivation, like if you look at swimmers,

439
00:19:40.038 --> 00:19:41.190
'cause swimmers are a good example,

440
00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:45.130
nobody swam into their late 20s no in the '70s or '80s.

441
00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:45.963
In amateur sport-

442
00:19:45.963 --> 00:19:46.796
There was no money.

443
00:19:46.796 --> 00:19:48.720
By the time you were 21 and you graduated college,

444
00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:49.553
that was it.

445
00:19:49.553 --> 00:19:50.386
You were done.

446
00:19:50.386 --> 00:19:53.160
Whereas now, there is a mechanism for doing that.

447
00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:55.880
So we're just extending the useful life of...

448
00:19:57.540 --> 00:20:00.020
What I don't understand

449
00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:04.050
is what's going on at the younger end, I don't understand.

450
00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:08.250
There's this kid, Erriyon Knighton,

451
00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:09.960
I think I'm mispronouncing his name,

452
00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:12.090
who's, what is he 19 or 18?

453
00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:15.960
Who's the second fastest sprinter in the world right now.

454
00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.970
We're seeing all these.

455
00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:20.250
There's an 800 meter runner in England

456
00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:23.550
who's running astonishing times in his late teens.

457
00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:25.500
And is Jakob Inhebritgtsen,

458
00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:26.940
the greatest middle distance runner in the world,

459
00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:27.840
who was...

460
00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:29.460
I don't understand, is it simply

461
00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:34.460
that we're bringing sophisticated training techniques

462
00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:36.570
earlier and earlier?

463
00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:39.270
Although that's not a satisfaction answer to me

464
00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:42.450
because it's not clear to me why that would work.

465
00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:45.000
Why wouldn't that just burn out runners earlier?

466
00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:46.980
Well, my frame for that is swimming.

467
00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:49.530
And when I see a 17-year-old break a world record

468
00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:51.690
that I just never thought would get broken,

469
00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:54.480
I think well, how many years of training

470
00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:55.770
could he have actually had

471
00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:57.813
that would be meaningfully so young?

472
00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:02.820
There are huge advances in training techniques.

473
00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:06.060
But then to your point, does that really account

474
00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:09.300
for that massive leap in performance

475
00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:12.600
or is the human species evolving?

476
00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:15.360
It's confusing, I don't know what the answer to that is.

477
00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:17.730
But it seems like there's no end in sight

478
00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.670
when it comes to that.

479
00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:22.410
Maybe the other thing I'd love to know

480
00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:26.230
is there's a denominator effect which is

481
00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:30.060
when you look at any of these phenomenon,

482
00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:33.010
we have to know what the size of the of the denominator is.

483
00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:36.450
And maybe the denominator,

484
00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:38.340
that is the number of people participating,

485
00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:40.350
entering a sport at the bottom end

486
00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:42.720
is growing much faster than we think.

487
00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:46.020
I did one of my podcasts this season in "Origins of history"

488
00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:49.173
is all about the relative age effect,

489
00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:54.030
both in education and in sport.

490
00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:59.030
This idea that without meaning to, in youth sports,

491
00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:04.740
the fact that there are age cutoffs in youth sports

492
00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:06.720
means that we unconsciously favor

493
00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:09.270
those who are relatively older in their age cohort.

494
00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:12.630
And in Australia, the most swimming mad country on earth,

495
00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:15.840
they did an analysis of their age class swimmers

496
00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:18.540
and realized because of the way

497
00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:20.313
they'd structured age cutoffs,

498
00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:24.990
there were no late maturing swimmers at all

499
00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:28.860
in the cohort of elite competitive age class swimmers.

500
00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:30.150
In other words, so they did a,

501
00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:33.180
there's a fascinating guy named Steven Coley.

502
00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:37.260
What he does is he takes all of the kids in Australia,

503
00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:40.830
men, boys doing 100 meter freestyle.

504
00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:42.450
And he does a very simple analysis.

505
00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:43.950
You can do a very simple analysis

506
00:22:43.950 --> 00:22:47.640
on adolescent boys and girls

507
00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:48.660
just by taking a series

508
00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:50.760
of seven or eight measurements of the body

509
00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:55.503
and figure out whether they are early matures,

510
00:22:56.580 --> 00:22:59.250
average matures, or late matures.

511
00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:03.509
So he takes a cohort of the 400 best freestyles

512
00:23:03.509 --> 00:23:05.580
age class swimmers in Australia,

513
00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:07.230
does this analysis on all of them,

514
00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:08.070
and figures out okay,

515
00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:11.730
which of these people are early, middle, and late.

516
00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:14.880
What he discovers is there are no late matures.

517
00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:19.800
So anyone who, just by pure chance, happens to be a kid

518
00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:23.040
who's a little bit developmentally behind his peers,

519
00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:27.420
they have left the sport of swimming by the age of 14 and 15

520
00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:29.070
they're gone, they got discouraged,

521
00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:30.600
they thought they weren't good, they didn't realize

522
00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:32.790
they were just behind three or four months.

523
00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:34.920
If you're behind three or four or five months at that age,

524
00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:36.300
of course, it's everything, right?

525
