00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:50.670
there were I think 152 race riots

788
00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:52.980
in the United States in 1967.

789
00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:57.570
It's where the optimism that accompanied

790
00:35:57.570 --> 00:36:00.090
Martin Luther King's version of civil rights

791
00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:03.903
dissipates in violence and rage.

792
00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:11.720
I think the summer of '67 is about as traumatic a summer

793
00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:15.000
as America has had in its history.

794
00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:16.950
We've sort of forgotten that now.

795
00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:19.200
So here we have a group of young black men

796
00:36:19.200 --> 00:36:21.390
who are the greatest sprinters in the world

797
00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:25.050
and who are very much immersed in what's going on in '67.

798
00:36:25.050 --> 00:36:29.140
And they're asking this question, "What is our obligation

799
00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:33.633
when we go to the Olympics in '68?

800
00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:37.500
The whole world's gonna be watching,

801
00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:40.800
we are the best in the world of what we do,

802
00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:45.780
we are young black men and America is in flames.

803
00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:50.757
Do we have an obligation to do something with our position?"

804
00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:53.880
And that is the amount of...

805
00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:58.230
We're talking about 19 and 20 year old young men

806
00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:01.680
who are being thrust onto the world stage

807
00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:05.520
and asked to defy the dominant ethos of the time

808
00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:09.690
about how an athlete ought to conduct themselves.

809
00:37:09.690 --> 00:37:14.690
It was explicitly against the Olympic ethos

810
00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:20.100
to bring politics at all to the games.

811
00:37:20.100 --> 00:37:23.400
Yeah, we are newer to this idea,

812
00:37:23.400 --> 00:37:28.267
that athletes can be activists and can flex their profile

813
00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:33.390
in order to advance social change.

814
00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:35.850
But this was not part of the landscape at the time,

815
00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:37.470
in fact, it was an anathema to it.

816
00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:41.640
Especially when you consider the power that was wielded

817
00:37:41.640 --> 00:37:44.400
by Avery Brundage and this whole notion

818
00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:47.100
that the Olympics should be completely separate

819
00:37:47.100 --> 00:37:51.090
from any kind of political statement whatsoever.

820
00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:56.090
Brundage, the series, he's the villain of the series.

821
00:37:56.620 --> 00:37:58.527
Well, he's an easy target, for sure.

822
00:37:58.527 --> 00:37:59.360
He's a Bond.
The guy was like...

823
00:37:59.360 --> 00:38:01.473
Yeah, he's a Bond villain as you say.

824
00:38:02.610 --> 00:38:03.750
He's unbelievable.

825
00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:07.800
And he runs the Olympics for 35 years.

826
00:38:07.800 --> 00:38:11.040
The modern Olympics is really created by him.

827
00:38:11.040 --> 00:38:16.040
And to say he has reprehensible views and retrograde views

828
00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:23.100
about the role of sport in society is to understate it.

829
00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:24.990
So he very strongly believes for example

830
00:38:24.990 --> 00:38:28.020
that Rhodesian South Africa, at the time that they have

831
00:38:28.020 --> 00:38:30.210
white supremacist regimes running them,

832
00:38:30.210 --> 00:38:33.810
ought to be welcome at the games, why?

833
00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:35.820
In part because he's sympathetic to what they do.

834
00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:39.300
But largely because but his stated belief is,

835
00:38:39.300 --> 00:38:42.960
a country's politics are irrelevant when it comes to sports.

836
00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:45.900
That it is not the role of the Olympic games

837
00:38:45.900 --> 00:38:46.733
to pass judgment

838
00:38:46.733 --> 00:38:50.490
on any participating country's internal policies.

839
00:38:50.490 --> 00:38:52.800
We're above that, we're sports.

840
00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:55.440
We're about young men and women

841
00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:59.040
who are embracing the amateur ideal.

842
00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:04.020
And on this shining city in the hill,

843
00:39:04.020 --> 00:39:06.930
doing our best on the field or the track

844
00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:11.930
and then going home and going back to their ordinary life.

845
00:39:13.290 --> 00:39:14.430
That's his model.

846
00:39:14.430 --> 00:39:17.040
His model is Roger Banister, who breaks the four minute mile

847
00:39:17.040 --> 00:39:18.630
and then goes back to medical school

848
00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:19.463
and never says a peep

849
00:39:19.463 --> 00:39:21.360
about the larger world of which he's a part.

850
00:39:21.360 --> 00:39:25.740
And God forbid he should ever profit off of his prowess

851
00:39:25.740 --> 00:39:26.610
Exactly.

852
00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:28.680
That's Brundage, that's what he believes.

853
00:39:28.680 --> 00:39:31.890
And sort of a backdrop to that and the history,

854
00:39:31.890 --> 00:39:33.570
I watched "The Stand" last night,

855
00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:36.630
the documentary about this very subject matter.

856
00:39:36.630 --> 00:39:38.400
And what I didn't understand,

857
00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:41.430
is the legacy of this dating back to,

858
00:39:41.430 --> 00:39:44.340
and you talk about this in the series as well,

859
00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:49.340
Brundage's role back in 1936, the Olympics then.

860
00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:53.100
And there was this whole consideration at the time

861
00:39:53.100 --> 00:39:55.560
as to whether the United States should boycott.

862
00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:57.510
And they have this meeting at the New York Athletic Club.

863
00:39:57.510 --> 00:39:58.343
But of course

864
00:39:58.343 --> 00:40:02.640
Jews are not allowed to enter the building there.

865
00:40:02.640 --> 00:40:05.280
So this vote was cast about whether or not to boycott,

866
00:40:05.280 --> 00:40:07.083
and of course there's no boycott.

867
00:40:08.670 --> 00:40:13.670
So as you said, he's been doing this for a long time.

868
00:40:13.860 --> 00:40:17.460
Brundage is the guy sent by the American Olympic Committee

869
00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:20.190
to Germany to figure out whether it would be appropriate

870
00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:22.740
to attend the '36 games in Berlin.

871
00:40:22.740 --> 00:40:24.750
And he comes back and says, "Yeah, it's fine."

872
00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:26.790
Now, this brings up

873
00:40:26.790 --> 00:40:29.040
an interesting philosophical point though.

874
00:40:29.040 --> 00:40:31.590
I actually think it was appropriate

875
00:40:31.590 --> 00:40:33.870
for America to attend the '36 games

876
00:40:33.870 --> 00:40:35.760
even though Adolf Hitler was intending

877
00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:39.150
those games to be a showcase for his Nazi state.

878
00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:40.530
And the reason I think it was appropriate

879
00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:45.530
was that we went there and Jesse Owens and a bunch of others

880
00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:48.930
kicked everyone's ass and stole the show.

881
00:40:48.930 --> 00:40:53.850
And Hitler had this vision of aryan supremacy

882
00:40:53.850 --> 00:40:57.990
and a black guy from Ohio shows up and dominates the games.

883
00:40:57.990 --> 00:41:00.360
So it's like, be careful what you wish for

884
00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:01.683
if you're Adolf Hitler.

885
00:41:02.580 --> 00:41:04.443
And I think the same thing happens,

886
00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:10.550
the same question was given to the athletes in '68.

887
00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:14.730
Originally the idea was that black athletes in America

888
00:41:14.730 --> 00:41:16.230
would boycott the '68 games.

889
00:41:16.230 --> 00:41:18.180
And they said, "Actually you know what,

890
00:41:18.180 --> 00:41:22.950
it's better that we go and use that platform to make a stand

891
00:41:22.950 --> 00:41:26.007
than to stay home and turn our back on the institution."

892
00:41:27.450 --> 00:41:30.930
I think in both cases going and making a statement

893
00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:32.330
is superior to staying home.

894
00:41:33.930 --> 00:41:36.100
I completely agree with the way

895
00:41:38.010 --> 00:41:40.290
we ended up in both those cases.

896
00:41:40.290 --> 00:41:43.080
You have a whole episode where you pose this choice

897
00:41:43.080 --> 00:41:45.930
like voice, exit, or loyalty.

898
00:41:45.930 --> 00:41:49.620
So go use your voice, exit, meaning boycott,

899
00:41:49.620 --> 00:41:52.470
or the Brundage model of just be loyal,

900
00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:54.810
be quiet and do your job.

901
00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:58.500
And it seems to me that when you canvas history,

902
00:41:58.500 --> 00:42:01.863
voice ends up being the most powerful over time.

903
00:42:02.764 --> 00:42:06.300
There's collateral damage in the short term.

904
00:42:06.300 --> 00:42:09.270
But ultimately with distance,

905
00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:12.900
you see the impact of that resonate and becoming.

906
00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:14.280
When we look at that photograph

907
00:42:14.280 --> 00:42:17.850
that is the touchdown of this whole series,

908
00:42:17.850 --> 00:42:21.480
nobody hasn't seen that image,

909
00:42:21.480 --> 00:42:24.810
it just resonates across decades.

910
00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:27.660
No, it ended up being a kind of...

911
00:42:27.660 --> 00:42:28.500
Yeah, I agree with you.

912
00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:31.680
I think Kareem Abdul-Jabbar

913
00:42:31.680 --> 00:42:35.880
famous does stay home in '68, boycotts.

914
00:42:35.880 --> 00:42:37.980
And I would really love to talk to him

915
00:42:37.980 --> 00:42:40.420
to see whether he regrets that decision

916
00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:45.053
as an old man now.

917
00:42:45.053 --> 00:42:47.280
Or if we look at the 1980 Olympic boycott,

918
00:42:47.280 --> 00:42:49.083
did that really achieve anything?

919
00:42:50.285 --> 00:42:51.118
Yeah, I think that all it did

920
00:42:51.118 --> 00:42:53.440
I think was rob a group of athletes of a chance

921
00:42:54.570 --> 00:42:59.570
to have gone to Moscow at the height of the Soviet Union

922
00:42:59.940 --> 00:43:02.523
and to have dominated on the track.

923
00:43:03.420 --> 00:43:04.680
That would have...

924
00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:05.513
When we think about it,

925
00:43:05.513 --> 00:43:09.570
the domestic audience for the Moscow games in 1980

926
00:43:09.570 --> 00:43:11.280
were a group of people who had been told

927
00:43:11.280 --> 00:43:13.590
the West was weak and decadent.

928
00:43:13.590 --> 00:43:17.010
And because of that, that's what justified the Soviet regime

929
00:43:17.010 --> 00:43:20.190
that they were pursuing a better model of how...

930
00:43:20.190 --> 00:43:21.240
But to have shown up

931
00:43:21.240 --> 00:43:26.190
and to have put that notion to that lie to the test

932
00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:27.900
would have been incredibly powerful.

933
00:43:27.900 --> 00:43:29.670
Yeah, interesting.

934
00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:31.800
I have friends that were on that '80 team.

935
00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:32.853
And to this day-

936
00:43:33.690 --> 00:43:35.010
Who do you know who was in there?

937
00:43:35.010 --> 00:43:36.060
My friend John Moffitt,

938
00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:37.620
he was the youngest member

939
00:43:37.620 --> 00:43:40.020
of the 1980 Olympic swimming team.

940
00:43:40.020 --> 00:43:41.310
He made the team in the 100 breast,

941
00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:43.800
I think he was 16 years old at the time.

942
00:43:43.800 --> 00:43:45.240
He ended up making,

943
00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:47.820
there's a weird parallel with Tommy in this,

944
00:43:47.820 --> 00:43:52.820
he ends up making the '84 team and sets the world record

945
00:43:53.790 --> 00:43:56.310
at Olympic trials in the 100 breaststroke.

946
00:43:56.310 --> 00:43:57.990
And in the pre-Olimps,

947
00:43:57.990 --> 00:44:01.350
he swims the Olympic record to qualify first.

948
00:44:01.350 --> 00:44:05.370
But he pulls his groin muscle and ultimately ends up

949
00:44:05.370 --> 00:44:07.350
I think fourth or six or something like that,

950
00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:09.060
Steve Linquist wins.

951
00:44:09.060 --> 00:44:12.810
So it's a mirror image, the converse of what happens

952
00:44:12.810 --> 00:44:15.333
with Tommy in the 200 meters in '68.

953
00:44:16.303 --> 00:44:19.800
Well, you bring up one of the many incredible side stories

954
00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:24.510
of the 200 meters of this series "Legacy of Speed"

955
00:44:24.510 --> 00:44:28.697
is Tommy Smith, then the greatest 200 meter runner

956
00:44:28.697 --> 00:44:32.760
in the world in the semis, as you say, pulls a groin.

957
00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:36.540
And first of all, if you pull a groin-

958
00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:37.500
You're done.

959
00:44:37.500 --> 00:44:38.333
You're done.

960
00:44:38.333 --> 00:44:39.930
I watched the documentary last night,

961
00:44:39.930 --> 00:44:43.057
you see him noticeably limping and you're like,

962
00:44:43.057 --> 00:44:44.273
"This guy has no chance."
No chance.

963
00:44:44.273 --> 00:44:46.800
Because it's not like he has a couple of days

964
00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:49.020
in between the semis and the final,

965
00:44:49.020 --> 00:44:50.746
he's got a couple of hours.
Hours

966
00:44:50.746 --> 00:44:53.430
And Bud Winter, this legendary coach,

967
00:44:53.430 --> 00:44:55.293
it's the greatest act of coaching.

968
00:44:56.370 --> 00:44:59.010
His entire career, Tommy Smith has been a disciple

969
00:44:59.010 --> 00:45:01.270
of Bud Winter's notions about relaxation

970
00:45:03.064 --> 00:45:05.730
and engaging the mental

971
00:45:05.730 --> 00:45:07.800
and psychological aspect of the sport.

972
00:45:07.800 --> 00:45:11.220
And Winter and Tommy Smith retreat

973
00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:16.220
and Smith prepares himself psychologically and emotionally

974
00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:20.280
for that final in such a way that

975
00:45:20.280 --> 00:45:23.400
not only is he capable of running with a pulled groin,

976
00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:24.990
he breaks the world record,

977
00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:28.350
a world record that would last I think 20 years.

978
00:45:28.350 --> 00:45:31.620
And he pulls up in the last five or 10 meters,

979
00:45:31.620 --> 00:45:33.389
he eases off.

980
00:45:33.389 --> 00:45:34.305
It's insane.
It's crazy.

981
00:45:34.305 --> 00:45:35.850
It's insane.

982
00:45:35.850 --> 00:45:36.796
Once you know that he-

983
00:45:36.796 --> 00:45:38.173
And he comes from behind too.

984
00:45:38.173 --> 00:45:39.713
The whole thing's insane, this the whole thing's insane.

985
00:45:40.620 --> 00:45:42.870
John Carlos is interesting.

986
00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:46.710
John Carlos, it's like I have a special affection for Carlos

987
00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:51.710
because like me, he has a Jamaican mom.

988
00:45:51.870 --> 00:45:55.830
And he's part of the West Indian diaspora to New York City.

989
00:45:55.830 --> 00:45:58.110
And he's so Jamaican in some,

990
00:45:58.110 --> 00:46:00.480
I realize I'm engaging in cultural stereotypes here,

991
00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:05.480
but he's just a firebrand,

992
00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:09.210
he's the kind of outspoken-

993
00:46:09.210 --> 00:46:13.110
More charismatic.
Charismatic performer one.

994
00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:15.300
And it doesn't really happen.

995
00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:16.500
I talk a little bit in the series

996
00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:19.410
about how you need to have a John Carlos

997
00:46:19.410 --> 00:46:20.780
if you're ever gonna do...

998
00:46:23.130 --> 00:46:25.260
The construction of these iconic moments,

999
00:46:25.260 --> 00:46:28.590
invariably includes a John Carlos like figure,

1000
00:46:28.590 --> 00:46:31.290
someone who has the, we were talking earlier

1001
00:46:31.290 --> 00:46:33.810
about that liberal of imagination,

1002
00:46:33.810 --> 00:46:35.070
someone who has the imagination

1003
00:46:35.070 --> 00:46:37.870
to see the possibilities of a moment like that.

1004
00:46:37.870 --> 00:46:40.980
So we have Tommy Smith, we've got John Carlos,

1005
00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:43.110
they both compete in the 200 meters.

1006
00:46:43.110 --> 00:46:47.190
The backdrop to that is all of these discussions

1007
00:46:47.190 --> 00:46:50.193
leading up to the Olympics, about whether to boycott or not,

1008
00:46:52.470 --> 00:46:54.060
they decide to go.

1009
00:46:54.060 --> 00:46:57.330
What was interesting about John Carlos is in the interim,

1010
00:46:57.330 --> 00:47:00.090
at some period leading up to the Olympic games,

1011
00:47:00.090 --> 00:47:01.710
he goes back to Harlem

1012
00:47:01.710 --> 00:47:05.040
and he has this meeting with Martin Luther King Jr.

1013
00:47:05.040 --> 00:47:08.910
Who encourages him to go and use that moment for his voice.

1014
00:47:08.910 --> 00:47:12.480
And of course we have Professor Harry Edwards

1015
00:47:12.480 --> 00:47:15.423
who's preparing these young athletes.

1016
00:47:17.580 --> 00:47:19.710
He seems to be the visionary in all of this,

1017
00:47:19.710 --> 00:47:23.460
knowing you guys are young, maybe you don't even realize

1018
00:47:23.460 --> 00:47:25.920
how seismic this could possibly be.

1019
00:47:25.920 --> 00:47:29.040
If things break in your favor and you win gold medals

1020
00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:31.530
or you're on that podium, you have this opportunity,

1021
00:47:31.530 --> 00:47:33.390
how are you gonna use that?

1022
00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:35.580
So they go, they compete,

1023
00:47:35.580 --> 00:47:38.430
Tommy ends up winning the gold medal

1024
00:47:38.430 --> 00:47:39.930
despite the groin and all of that,

1025
00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:41.520
Carlos is third.

1026
00:47:41.520 --> 00:47:43.590
And what was interesting about the way

1027
00:47:43.590 --> 00:47:46.350
that you unfurl this aspect of this story

1028
00:47:46.350 --> 00:47:49.380
is that despite all of the energy behind the scenes,

1029
00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:50.430
they still hadn't figured out

1030
00:47:50.430 --> 00:47:53.940
what they were gonna do or not do until the last minute.

1031
00:47:53.940 --> 00:47:57.150
They're under the stadium working out,

1032
00:47:57.150 --> 00:47:58.890
we gotta do something.

1033
00:47:58.890 --> 00:48:01.380
And although, it's funny all of them,

1034
00:48:01.380 --> 00:48:03.540
as is so often the case in these kinds of histories,

1035
00:48:03.540 --> 00:48:05.760
everyone has their own version of what happens.

1036
00:48:05.760 --> 00:48:07.720
But they did bring black socks

1037
00:48:08.760 --> 00:48:12.420
and black gloves with them to Mexico.

1038
00:48:12.420 --> 00:48:14.067
And little they had this-

1039
00:48:14.067 --> 00:48:15.840
But didn't Tommy have to tell his wife

1040
00:48:15.840 --> 00:48:18.330
after he had already arrived there and she was coming later

1041
00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:20.760
like, bring the gloves?

1042
00:48:20.760 --> 00:48:22.770
They're clearly thinking about

1043
00:48:22.770 --> 00:48:26.463
what kind of symbolic statement makes the most sense.

1044
00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:30.390
And by the end, they're wearing a scarf, so the scarf...

1045
00:48:30.390 --> 00:48:33.870
The socks symbolize the working man.

1046
00:48:33.870 --> 00:48:34.703
Beads.

1047
00:48:34.703 --> 00:48:37.830
Their jackets are open because-

1048
00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:38.760
I didn't know any of this.

1049
00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:41.430
You know glove, but you don't know any of the other stuff.

1050
00:48:41.430 --> 00:48:44.193
There's like five or six different things they're doing

1051
00:48:44.193 --> 00:48:49.193
that all have a very specific symbolic purpose.

1052
00:48:50.640 --> 00:48:55.640
It's this weirdly complex coded act that is, again yeah...

1053
00:48:58.980 --> 00:49:01.930
All I knew, I just thought it was a black glove in the air.

1054
00:49:02.814 --> 00:49:05.220
And the other amazing story,

1055
00:49:05.220 --> 00:49:08.640
so the guy who wins the silver in the 200

1056
00:49:08.640 --> 00:49:11.730
is this guy, Peter Norman, who's an Australian, white guy.

1057
00:49:11.730 --> 00:49:14.567
And they're backstage and Smith and Carlos

1058
00:49:15.543 --> 00:49:16.713
who win the gold and the bronze,

1059
00:49:16.713 --> 00:49:17.610
are talking about what they wanna do.

1060
00:49:17.610 --> 00:49:20.250
And Norman says, "Oh, can I be a part of this?"

1061
00:49:20.250 --> 00:49:21.127
And of course the other guy is like,

1062
00:49:21.127 --> 00:49:23.010
"Wait, you're a white guy from Australia."

1063
00:49:23.010 --> 00:49:25.890
And Norman says, "Well, actually my family

1064
00:49:25.890 --> 00:49:28.860
is deeply involved with the Salvation Army.

1065
00:49:28.860 --> 00:49:33.330
We've been social justice pioneers my whole life,

1066
00:49:33.330 --> 00:49:35.430
your cause is something I believe in."

1067
00:49:35.430 --> 00:49:37.350
So they go and they find him a little,

1068
00:49:37.350 --> 00:49:42.083
they're all wearing these pins that symbolize their cause.

1069
00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:44.970
Norman borrows a pin and puts it on.

1070
00:49:44.970 --> 00:49:49.590
So he's not raising a fist but he's also participating.

1071
00:49:49.590 --> 00:49:51.930
And the effect of him wearing a pin

1072
00:49:51.930 --> 00:49:56.760
is that he is banned from the Australian Olympic team,

1073
00:49:56.760 --> 00:50:00.600
he does not get to go in 1972, they don't even invite him.

1074
00:50:00.600 --> 00:50:02.250
Even though to this day

1075
00:50:02.250 --> 00:50:05.820
he is the Australian record holder in the 200 meters,

1076
00:50:05.820 --> 00:50:08.610
they don't even invite him to the Sydney games.

1077
00:50:08.610 --> 00:50:10.080
He's just banished from the sport

1078
00:50:10.080 --> 00:50:12.900
because he chose to participate in this.

1079
00:50:12.900 --> 00:50:17.280
He really never recovers and he ends up dying.

1080
00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:19.263
He died several years ago.

1081
00:50:20.155 --> 00:50:23.163
And I think Tommy and John went and were his pole-bearers.

1082
00:50:24.990 --> 00:50:26.490
There's a lovely kind of-

1083
00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:31.470
Well, we look at this courageous act

1084
00:50:31.470 --> 00:50:35.523
in an era in which courage is hard to come by.

1085
00:50:36.532 --> 00:50:37.830
I don't know that we could ever really imagine

1086
00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:39.330
just how courageous it was for them

1087
00:50:39.330 --> 00:50:41.400
to do that at that moment.

1088
00:50:41.400 --> 00:50:46.080
And we think, what an amazing symbolic act

1089
00:50:46.080 --> 00:50:49.890
on behalf of of civil rights and we tend to overlook

1090
00:50:49.890 --> 00:50:53.310
or we don't understand the fallout from that.

1091
00:50:53.310 --> 00:50:54.660
We have Brundage

1092
00:50:54.660 --> 00:50:57.630
who basically gets rid of these guys immediately.

1093
00:50:57.630 --> 00:51:00.000
And then these guys go on to have a really hard time

1094
00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:01.170
for a very long time.

1095
00:51:01.170 --> 00:51:05.460
It's only many decades later that we can appreciate them

1096
00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:09.960
and erect statues and celebrate them.

1097
00:51:09.960 --> 00:51:13.380
But their lives were decimated as a result of this

1098
00:51:13.380 --> 00:51:14.463
in many ways.

1099
00:51:16.966 --> 00:51:20.310
The way the popular press reacted to that,

1100
00:51:20.310 --> 00:51:24.843
in the moment to that protest, was in retrospect vicious.

1101
00:51:26.280 --> 00:51:30.480
People thought they were outlaws, that they had...

1102
00:51:30.480 --> 00:51:34.620
Avery Brundage referred to what Carlos and Tommy Smith

1103
00:51:34.620 --> 00:51:38.223
did on the podium as a violent act.

1104
00:51:39.870 --> 00:51:41.550
He would in fact in 1972,

1105
00:51:41.550 --> 00:51:43.950
when there was an actual violent act,

1106
00:51:43.950 --> 00:51:47.640
when the Israeli Olympic team was slaughtered by terrorists,

1107
00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:52.640
Brundage famously refereed to those in the same breath,

1108
00:51:54.450 --> 00:51:58.320
those two acts, an actual terrorist attack

1109
00:51:58.320 --> 00:52:01.950
and two young men holding up their fist on a victory stand,

1110
00:52:01.950 --> 00:52:05.213
as violent assaults on the Olympic dream.

1111
00:52:09.150 --> 00:52:12.600
And then they come home and sports writers turn on them,

1112
00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:14.073
the public turns on them.

1113
00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:17.100
What's weird, of course, about that

1114
00:52:17.100 --> 00:52:21.303
of course is that it brings up Collin Kaepernick.

1115
00:52:22.380 --> 00:52:24.690
Who was also advised by Harry Edwards

1116
00:52:24.690 --> 00:52:26.190
and he was acting in his protest

1117
00:52:26.190 --> 00:52:29.157
very much in the spirit of Smith and Carlos in '68.

1118
00:52:29.157 --> 00:52:32.040
And the same thing happens to Kaepernick.

1119
00:52:32.040 --> 00:52:35.100
We think we're beyond that, we think we've somehow...

1120
00:52:35.100 --> 00:52:37.230
But Kaepernick, he has more support

1121
00:52:37.230 --> 00:52:38.940
than Smith and Carlos do in '68.

1122
00:52:38.940 --> 00:52:43.020
But the NFL establishment turns its back on him,

1123
00:52:43.020 --> 00:52:44.700
pushes him out of the sport.

1124
00:52:44.700 --> 00:52:47.793
It makes you think, have we really come that far,

1125
00:52:49.759 --> 00:52:53.790
that change is slow and hard wrote in so many ways.

1126
00:52:53.790 --> 00:52:58.790
I think with Kaepernick, I feel like there's a division.

1127
00:52:59.640 --> 00:53:02.820
There are plenty of people who are supportive of what he did

1128
00:53:02.820 --> 00:53:05.703
but not enough for him to be playing in the NFL.

1129
00:53:07.140 --> 00:53:08.370
It's interesting that thread.

1130
00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:10.890
And the same way that you can't understand Usain Bolt,

1131
00:53:10.890 --> 00:53:14.520
you can't really understand contextually Colin Kaepernick

1132
00:53:14.520 --> 00:53:16.653
without understanding this story.

1133
00:53:18.480 --> 00:53:19.330
No, it's funny.

1134
00:53:22.950 --> 00:53:26.130
It's funny even though I say I'm a a track and field fan,

1135
00:53:26.130 --> 00:53:30.270
I hadn't made the connection between Kaepernick and '68.

1136
00:53:30.270 --> 00:53:34.290
That he's very self-consciously participating

1137
00:53:34.290 --> 00:53:38.583
in that tradition of silent protest.

1138
00:53:44.370 --> 00:53:47.700
I don't know even the interesting question to ask Kaepernick

1139
00:53:47.700 --> 00:53:50.640
I've never heard, I'm sure he has an answer, I know,

1140
00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:52.530
is whether he anticipated

1141
00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:55.503
that he would face as much backlash as he did.

1142
00:53:56.670 --> 00:53:58.980
Was he doing that on the assumption

1143
00:53:58.980 --> 00:54:03.980
that the contemporary world was very different in '68,

1144
00:54:05.073 --> 00:54:08.070
that his act would have new meaning today

1145
00:54:08.070 --> 00:54:10.380
or did he think he would face the same kind of firestorm

1146
00:54:10.380 --> 00:54:11.790
as Smith and Carlos?

1147
00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:12.623
I don't know what he-

1148
00:54:12.623 --> 00:54:14.269
Did Did you try to reach out to him for this?

1149
00:54:14.269 --> 00:54:16.290
No, I didn't.

1150
00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:18.660
We were very much in this,

1151
00:54:18.660 --> 00:54:21.090
we talk about Kaepernick glancingly at the end.

1152
00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:24.630
But we were trying in this podcast to stay very focused

1153
00:54:24.630 --> 00:54:28.353
on the events leading up to '68.

1154
00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:31.980
I think it's sometimes better in these kinds of stories

1155
00:54:31.980 --> 00:54:35.190
to let the listener draw their own conclusions

1156
00:54:35.190 --> 00:54:39.983
about how these themes resonate today.

1157
00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:46.500
You mentioned that pin that Peter Norman adorned.

1158
00:54:46.500 --> 00:54:49.140
That pin was for this organization

1159
00:54:49.140 --> 00:54:52.022
called the Olympic Project for Human Rights, right?

1160
00:54:52.022 --> 00:54:52.855
Yes, yes.

1161
00:54:52.855 --> 00:54:55.110
And there's a whole story there as well

1162
00:54:55.110 --> 00:54:57.180
that involves the Harvard crew team,

1163
00:54:57.180 --> 00:54:58.826
which I thought was really interesting.

1164
00:54:58.826 --> 00:55:00.720
(both laughing)

1165
00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:02.490
They're trying to find a pin for Norman

1166
00:55:02.490 --> 00:55:04.080
and no one has an extra one.

1167
00:55:04.080 --> 00:55:07.470
But it turns out the Harvard crew team or the crew team

1168
00:55:07.470 --> 00:55:09.060
which is made up of many people from Harvard,

1169
00:55:09.060 --> 00:55:10.350
they have pins.

1170
00:55:10.350 --> 00:55:12.157
So Tommy Smith goes over to them and says,

1171
00:55:12.157 --> 00:55:14.610
"Can we borrow a pin for Norman?

1172
00:55:14.610 --> 00:55:17.360
Our friend here wants to wear it on the victory stand."

1173
00:55:19.320 --> 00:55:23.670
Who wouldn't have loved to have been backstage

1174
00:55:23.670 --> 00:55:26.340
leading up to in those in those moments?

1175
00:55:26.340 --> 00:55:28.320
And then the other great, fantastic fact

1176
00:55:28.320 --> 00:55:30.330
about the medal ceremony that day

1177
00:55:30.330 --> 00:55:33.330
is Brundage doesn't even show up.

1178
00:55:33.330 --> 00:55:37.380
So our villain who is always there

1179
00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:38.673
in the big moments of the Olympics,

1180
00:55:38.673 --> 00:55:40.680
this is what he lives for,

1181
00:55:40.680 --> 00:55:42.210
the Olympics is his creation.

1182
00:55:42.210 --> 00:55:43.860
If a world records broken,

1183
00:55:43.860 --> 00:55:45.330
he's there handing out the medals.

1184
00:55:45.330 --> 00:55:50.043
He's in Acapulco for the yachting competition.

1185
00:55:50.942 --> 00:55:52.032
Twirling his mustache.

1186
00:55:52.032 --> 00:55:52.890
Twirling his mustache.

1187
00:55:52.890 --> 00:55:55.680
He gets outta town, he wants to know part of...

1188
00:55:55.680 --> 00:55:59.883
'Cause they know something is up with Smith and Carlos.

1189
00:56:01.680 --> 00:56:06.630
It's so weird that really doing this series

1190
00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:11.630
really made me think hard about our notions about amateurism

1191
00:56:12.660 --> 00:56:16.500
and how did sport in those years,

1192
00:56:16.500 --> 00:56:18.720
and it still lingers today,

1193
00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:20.280
how do we come to think of sport

1194
00:56:20.280 --> 00:56:24.750
as something that ought to be separate

1195
00:56:24.750 --> 00:56:26.973
from the rest of society?

1196
00:56:29.370 --> 00:56:32.340
Why is it so shocking to us that Colin Kaepernick

1197
00:56:32.340 --> 00:56:35.520
would want to reflect at a football game

1198
00:56:35.520 --> 00:56:38.400
what was going on outside the stadium?

1199
00:56:38.400 --> 00:56:39.990
Why does that upset us?

1200
00:56:39.990 --> 00:56:41.040
Or why does that...

1201
00:56:41.040 --> 00:56:42.390
I don't get that.

1202
00:56:42.390 --> 00:56:47.390
There still is this legacy, this Brundage-esque legacy

1203
00:56:49.050 --> 00:56:52.800
of, these things should not be intertwined with each other.

1204
00:56:52.800 --> 00:56:55.650
What is the Genesis of that, this puritan ideal

1205
00:56:55.650 --> 00:57:00.650
that sports exist completely outside of any other ideas?

1206
00:57:01.980 --> 00:57:04.953
Part of it is, there is this notion,

1207
00:57:06.240 --> 00:57:10.320
and it is unconsciously I think racist in origin,

1208
00:57:10.320 --> 00:57:12.677
or maybe not racist is the wrong word,

1209
00:57:12.677 --> 00:57:15.120
'cause it applies both to blacks and whites.

1210
00:57:15.120 --> 00:57:18.753
There is a feeling that if you excel athletically,

1211
00:57:19.913 --> 00:57:23.769
you have somehow compromised your intellectual gifts,

1212
00:57:23.769 --> 00:57:25.203
that you can do one or the other.

1213
00:57:25.203 --> 00:57:28.980
That the person who strengthens their muscles

1214
00:57:28.980 --> 00:57:32.430
is at the same time weakening their mind.

1215
00:57:32.430 --> 00:57:37.430
That's a very old trope that we had about the dumb jock.

1216
00:57:39.060 --> 00:57:40.170
That's what that's about.

1217
00:57:40.170 --> 00:57:43.890
But of course, and you know this better than I do,

1218
00:57:43.890 --> 00:57:46.830
the modern athlete is the opposite of that.

1219
00:57:46.830 --> 00:57:48.660
That there is no way to succeed

1220
00:57:48.660 --> 00:57:51.300
at a high level in sports today

1221
00:57:51.300 --> 00:57:54.480
if you are not intellectually sophisticated

1222
00:57:54.480 --> 00:57:57.813
and cognitively engaged in what you're doing.

1223
00:57:58.770 --> 00:58:01.170
You can't be a dumb jock and a good jock today,

1224
00:58:01.170 --> 00:58:05.253
it's not possible, you can't be.

1225
00:58:08.010 --> 00:58:11.520
Do you think LeBron James isn't a brilliant guy?

1226
00:58:11.520 --> 00:58:13.560
Of course he has to be.

1227
00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:14.940
He's done what no one else has done,

1228
00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:16.980
he's figured out all this stuff about

1229
00:58:16.980 --> 00:58:20.010
how to succeed at the high level for 20 years.

1230
00:58:20.010 --> 00:58:23.430
Employing teams of people and spending millions of dollars

1231
00:58:23.430 --> 00:58:25.440
to ensure his success,

1232
00:58:25.440 --> 00:58:27.900
by surrounding himself with the smartest people

1233
00:58:27.900 --> 00:58:31.345
to make him the smartest athlete that he can possibly be.

1234
00:58:31.345 --> 00:58:33.600
And the amount of discipline that's necessary

1235
00:58:33.600 --> 00:58:36.360
in any kind of sport at a high level now.

1236
00:58:36.360 --> 00:58:37.680
We've always known that discipline

1237
00:58:37.680 --> 00:58:42.513
is a very close corollary of cognitive sophistication.

1238
00:58:44.160 --> 00:58:46.500
So I don't know why that idea,

1239
00:58:46.500 --> 00:58:48.570
but it does persist in curious ways.

1240
00:58:48.570 --> 00:58:49.980
Shut up and dribble.

1241
00:58:49.980 --> 00:58:51.080
Shut up and dribble.

1242
00:58:52.080 --> 00:58:54.090
How do we transcend that?

1243
00:58:54.090 --> 00:58:59.040
Only through these types of transgressive acts, I think.

1244
00:58:59.040 --> 00:59:01.953
I don't see any other way past that.

1245
00:59:05.426 --> 00:59:07.770
It is interesting, this is such an interesting case study

1246
00:59:07.770 --> 00:59:09.633
'cause back to the Harvard crew team,

1247
00:59:10.560 --> 00:59:12.750
they had their Olympic trials I think in California

1248
00:59:12.750 --> 00:59:14.790
and then they went to go see Harry Edwards,

1249
00:59:14.790 --> 00:59:16.080
they wanted to...

1250
00:59:16.080 --> 00:59:17.340
These are all white guys

1251
00:59:17.340 --> 00:59:19.080
at the most privileged university in the world.

1252
00:59:19.080 --> 00:59:21.900
And they're like, "We wanna figure out a way to support you

1253
00:59:21.900 --> 00:59:23.850
and participate in this."

1254
00:59:23.850 --> 00:59:26.700
And Harry Edwards then went and visited them at Cambridge

1255
00:59:26.700 --> 00:59:28.620
and there's this press conference.

1256
00:59:28.620 --> 00:59:31.380
So there were people

1257
00:59:31.380 --> 00:59:34.380
who were on the right page of history with this,

1258
00:59:34.380 --> 00:59:37.920
and yet there's so much further to go.

1259
00:59:37.920 --> 00:59:39.480
We have Rule 50 right now,

1260
00:59:39.480 --> 00:59:42.000
which was debated leading into Tokyo.

1261
00:59:42.000 --> 00:59:44.970
And they made these tiny concessions that are confusing.

1262
00:59:44.970 --> 00:59:47.457
I dunno if you've read through the amendments to it.

1263
00:59:47.457 --> 00:59:50.730
And I'm like, I don't even really know what to make of this.

1264
00:59:50.730 --> 00:59:54.300
It's still very much Avery inspired

1265
00:59:54.300 --> 00:59:56.190
in terms of what you can and can't do.

1266
00:59:56.190 --> 00:59:59.250
Rule 50 is the part of the Olympic charter

1267
00:59:59.250 --> 01:00:03.450
that says essentially you can't bring politics to the games.

1268
01:00:03.450 --> 01:00:05.820
And we'll let you do a little bit here and there.

1269
01:00:05.820 --> 01:00:08.220
But just as long as it's not during competition

1270
01:00:08.220 --> 01:00:11.310
or during any of the anthems or on the podium

1271
01:00:11.310 --> 01:00:14.253
or any of the places where it would be the most impactful.

1272
01:00:15.390 --> 01:00:20.390
Can you imagine if we had an Olympics this summer

1273
01:00:20.460 --> 01:00:22.320
and the Ukrainian athlete,

1274
01:00:22.320 --> 01:00:25.980
there's a brilliant Ukrainian high jumper woman.

1275
01:00:25.980 --> 01:00:30.930
If she wins the gold medal, what a moment for someone

1276
01:00:30.930 --> 01:00:33.813
to make some kind of symbolic gesture.

1277
01:00:34.950 --> 01:00:36.753
Why would we deny ourselves?

1278
01:00:37.590 --> 01:00:38.950
By the way, parenthetically

1279
01:00:41.280 --> 01:00:44.490
the Olympics is primarily for the general public,

1280
01:00:44.490 --> 01:00:45.633
it's entertainment,

1281
01:00:47.010 --> 01:00:50.070
why are they trying to make this sport less interesting?

1282
01:00:50.070 --> 01:00:52.260
But there is something so uniquely special

1283
01:00:52.260 --> 01:00:55.020
about the Olympics, and there always has been.

1284
01:00:55.020 --> 01:00:57.720
I grew up obsessed with the Olympics,

1285
01:00:57.720 --> 01:01:01.560
watching all those fantastic but greenspan movies

1286
01:01:01.560 --> 01:01:03.780
with the monotone flat voiceover

1287
01:01:03.780 --> 01:01:08.780
and just riveted by the elevated notion

1288
01:01:09.420 --> 01:01:11.010
of that Olympic ideal.

1289
01:01:11.010 --> 01:01:16.010
Which I guess does have some Avery Brundage roots in it.

1290
01:01:16.530 --> 01:01:21.090
Like this is separate from normal humans,

1291
01:01:21.090 --> 01:01:22.293
what's going on here.

1292
01:01:23.880 --> 01:01:27.360
And I think that that creates that high energy crucible

1293
01:01:27.360 --> 01:01:30.420
for making some kind of statement.

1294
01:01:30.420 --> 01:01:34.710
Because it will resonate more than your average NFL game.

1295
01:01:34.710 --> 01:01:36.960
Kaepernick is an outlier in that regard.

1296
01:01:36.960 --> 01:01:38.490
But in general,

1297
01:01:38.490 --> 01:01:41.283
all eyes are on the Olympics once every four years,

1298
01:01:42.600 --> 01:01:44.700
what are you gonna do in that moment?

1299
01:01:44.700 --> 01:01:45.533
It's funny,

1300
01:01:48.756 --> 01:01:50.790
I was that kid yeah Who was obsessed with the Olympics.

1301
01:01:50.790 --> 01:01:52.140
That has certainly waned.

1302
01:01:55.200 --> 01:01:59.883
I think it's become this absurd, bloated enterprise.

1303
01:02:00.717 --> 01:02:05.130
It's too unwieldy, it's too big, it costs too much money.

1304
01:02:05.130 --> 01:02:06.030
This idea that countries

1305
01:02:06.030 --> 01:02:08.280
are spending tens of billions of dollars

1306
01:02:08.280 --> 01:02:12.000
to stage an event the last three weeks, whatever it is

1307
01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:13.800
and then everyone goes home again,

1308
01:02:13.800 --> 01:02:15.570
there's just something that rubs me the wrong way.

1309
01:02:15.570 --> 01:02:19.410
I think it needs to be radically re-imagined,

1310
01:02:19.410 --> 01:02:23.880
it should be in one place or just rotate among three cities.

1311
01:02:23.880 --> 01:02:27.120
It needs to be broken up so it's more manageable.

1312
01:02:27.120 --> 01:02:28.490
It's just all kinds of...

1313
01:02:29.628 --> 01:02:34.407
I think it's a little bit out of control at the moment.

1314
01:02:34.407 --> 01:02:37.500
And the idea that we're having games

1315
01:02:37.500 --> 01:02:40.050
in places where the weather is completely inhospitable

1316
01:02:40.050 --> 01:02:44.760
to athletic performance, it's just nuts.

1317
01:02:44.760 --> 01:02:49.760
You're gonna have a marathon in Rio or or Japan

1318
01:02:49.950 --> 01:02:52.590
in the middle of the summer, are we nuts?

1319
01:02:52.590 --> 01:02:53.670
Well, if you're gonna talk about

1320
01:02:53.670 --> 01:02:56.610
extricating politics from the Olympics,

1321
01:02:56.610 --> 01:02:58.950
let's begin with how they select the cities,

1322
01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:02.373
is the most overtly political thing there is.

1323
01:03:03.570 --> 01:03:07.080
I think it should be in Oslo,

1324
01:03:07.080 --> 01:03:08.790
they have a ton of money the Norwegians.

1325
01:03:08.790 --> 01:03:10.140
I don't know if they have enough hotel rooms.

1326
01:03:10.140 --> 01:03:12.630
But the weather's nice and cool in the summer.

1327
01:03:12.630 --> 01:03:13.800
Stick it in Oslo and just say,

1328
01:03:13.800 --> 01:03:15.480
we're all going to Oslo every four years.

1329
01:03:15.480 --> 01:03:17.707
And shouldn't it just be in Athens for very summer again?

1330
01:03:17.707 --> 01:03:19.410
No, cause it's too hot, Rich.

1331
01:03:19.410 --> 01:03:22.470
Yeah, but that is the history of the games, right?

1332
01:03:22.470 --> 01:03:24.180
But you can make it impossible

1333
01:03:24.180 --> 01:03:26.373
for people to run their events.

1334
01:03:27.330 --> 01:03:30.510
Was it Mexico City in like October?

1335
01:03:30.510 --> 01:03:31.612
Yeah, it was pushed.

1336
01:03:31.612 --> 01:03:33.120
It was weird, it was later in the year.

1337
01:03:33.120 --> 01:03:36.210
Because they wanted the Mexico...

1338
01:03:36.210 --> 01:03:37.230
There's many reasons.

1339
01:03:37.230 --> 01:03:39.120
One rumored thing is the Mexican government

1340
01:03:39.120 --> 01:03:40.800
needed more time to get there.

1341
01:03:40.800 --> 01:03:43.710
The country was being racked by protests

1342
01:03:43.710 --> 01:03:46.560
and they wanted a chance to get it under control.

1343
01:03:46.560 --> 01:03:48.330
But I thought it was also-

1344
01:03:48.330 --> 01:03:50.370
Which has to do with the money being spent

1345
01:03:50.370 --> 01:03:53.070
on the Olympic games and not on the populace.

1346
01:03:53.070 --> 01:03:56.073
But would there be a weather reason?

1347
01:03:57.780 --> 01:03:59.580
No, because Mexico City's at altitude,

1348
01:03:59.580 --> 01:04:02.103
so not a terribly hot place in the summer.

1349
01:04:03.390 --> 01:04:04.800
Sorry to interrupt the flow,

1350
01:04:04.800 --> 01:04:06.390
we'll be right back with more awesome.

1351
