1383
01:05:34.950 --> 01:05:38.190 line:15%
All right, let's get back to the show.

1384
01:05:38.190 --> 01:05:39.840
The other character that we haven't talked about

1385
01:05:39.840 --> 01:05:44.840
is Lee Evans, and he creates a no win situation for himself

1386
01:05:45.030 --> 01:05:47.220
despite winning a gold medal.

1387
01:05:47.220 --> 01:05:52.220
Well, he had the bad fortune to come after the 200 meters.

1388
01:05:53.973 --> 01:05:55.560
So he's a 400 meter runner

1389
01:05:55.560 --> 01:05:59.280
and the world's greatest 400 meter runner at the time.

1390
01:05:59.280 --> 01:06:01.650
And his final is after the 200 meters.

1391
01:06:01.650 --> 01:06:06.120
So after basically Carlos and Tommy Smith

1392
01:06:06.120 --> 01:06:08.110
have burned the place down

1393
01:06:09.360 --> 01:06:12.330
and the whole world is in uproar over what they've done-

1394
01:06:12.330 --> 01:06:13.230
They're sent home.

1395
01:06:13.230 --> 01:06:15.130
They're sent home and Evans is left-

1396
01:06:16.410 --> 01:06:18.060
If they weren't sent home, the US team

1397
01:06:18.060 --> 01:06:20.599
was looking at possibly the entire team having to go.

1398
01:06:20.599 --> 01:06:21.660
Being expelled.

1399
01:06:21.660 --> 01:06:22.950
Would Brundage really have done that,

1400
01:06:22.950 --> 01:06:24.210
I haven't thought about that.

1401
01:06:24.210 --> 01:06:26.360
He would've destroyed the games to do that.

1402
01:06:28.290 --> 01:06:30.660
So everyone's looking at Smith's and is like, or at Evans

1403
01:06:30.660 --> 01:06:32.850
and is like, "All right Lee, you're up next,

1404
01:06:32.850 --> 01:06:34.650
you're part of the same group.

1405
01:06:34.650 --> 01:06:36.780
You're probably gonna win the gold medal,

1406
01:06:36.780 --> 01:06:38.580
What are you gonna do on the stand?"

1407
01:06:38.580 --> 01:06:43.580
And he has a full-fledged breakdown before the 400 meters.

1408
01:06:47.488 --> 01:06:51.960
This crowd of journalists mobbing him and there's like...

1409
01:06:51.960 --> 01:06:53.490
Bud Winter takes him aside

1410
01:06:53.490 --> 01:06:55.830
and does the same thing he had to do with Tommy Smith,

1411
01:06:55.830 --> 01:06:59.673
nurses him back to psychological health before the final.

1412
01:07:00.720 --> 01:07:05.720
And Bud Winter has this, he says turns to him at one point,

1413
01:07:06.600 --> 01:07:08.857
this is like hours before the final and said,

1414
01:07:08.857 --> 01:07:12.580
"Lee, you couldn't run to the corner store

1415
01:07:13.710 --> 01:07:15.930
to get a pint of milk right now."

1416
01:07:15.930 --> 01:07:20.520
That's how he's just overwhelmed

1417
01:07:20.520 --> 01:07:21.810
with the pressure of the moment.

1418
01:07:21.810 --> 01:07:23.580
And he takes him back to his room

1419
01:07:23.580 --> 01:07:26.340
and they go through all their meditative techniques

1420
01:07:26.340 --> 01:07:29.400
and he restores his state of mind.

1421
01:07:29.400 --> 01:07:31.710
And Evan goes out there and sets a world record

1422
01:07:31.710 --> 01:07:36.120
that would not be broken for I think more than 20 years.

1423
01:07:36.120 --> 01:07:38.880
It was one of the great track and field gold medals.

1424
01:07:38.880 --> 01:07:40.470
He just has a sensational performance.

1425
01:07:40.470 --> 01:07:42.150
And he does on the victory stand

1426
01:07:42.150 --> 01:07:46.833
a muted version of the protest that Smith and Carlos do.

1427
01:07:46.833 --> 01:07:48.483
He kind of finds a middle ground.

1428
01:07:49.500 --> 01:07:51.240
But it's just a kind of...

1429
01:07:51.240 --> 01:07:56.190
So he doesn't produce the iconic moment

1430
01:07:56.190 --> 01:07:57.540
that Smith and Carlos does.

1431
01:07:57.540 --> 01:08:01.710
But he doesn't have to because what he's saying is

1432
01:08:01.710 --> 01:08:06.710
he continues the tradition and makes it plain

1433
01:08:08.430 --> 01:08:11.463
that he's also making a statement.

1434
01:08:12.330 --> 01:08:14.040
A couple of observations on that.

1435
01:08:14.040 --> 01:08:16.110
First, the pressure alone

1436
01:08:16.110 --> 01:08:17.790
just to perform at the Olympic games

1437
01:08:17.790 --> 01:08:19.470
when you're expected to win a gold medal

1438
01:08:19.470 --> 01:08:22.140
is beyond what most of us can imagine.

1439
01:08:22.140 --> 01:08:24.570
And then to layer on top of that

1440
01:08:24.570 --> 01:08:26.430
all the political ramifications

1441
01:08:26.430 --> 01:08:30.000
of what he might or might not do on the podium,

1442
01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:32.313
how do you not have your head just explode?

1443
01:08:33.480 --> 01:08:35.530
They're worried they're gonna get shot,

1444
01:08:37.205 --> 01:08:40.020
they're getting death threats left and right.

1445
01:08:40.020 --> 01:08:41.610
They think that when he stand up on that podium,

1446
01:08:41.610 --> 01:08:44.880
someone's gonna take out a gun and a sniper is gonna...

1447
01:08:44.880 --> 01:08:46.710
They're really-
The thing with Tommy.

1448
01:08:46.710 --> 01:08:48.030
When he pulled his groin,

1449
01:08:48.030 --> 01:08:51.210
he thought that he might have been shot.

1450
01:08:51.210 --> 01:08:53.850
Yeah, that was a legit fear.

1451
01:08:53.850 --> 01:08:55.350
How crazy is that?

1452
01:08:55.350 --> 01:08:59.370
A legit fear for a runner in a race at the Olympics

1453
01:08:59.370 --> 01:09:02.160
is that someone's gonna take them out,

1454
01:09:02.160 --> 01:09:03.120
someone's gonna kill them.

1455
01:09:03.120 --> 01:09:05.823
But it takes us back to '68,

1456
01:09:06.900 --> 01:09:10.923
not only was the long hot summer in America '67,

1457
01:09:12.133 --> 01:09:13.860
where the whole country seemed like they were in flames.

1458
01:09:13.860 --> 01:09:16.590
But just weeks before the Olympic games in Mexico,

1459
01:09:16.590 --> 01:09:18.093
the city was in lockdown,

1460
01:09:19.241 --> 01:09:21.630
there was a massacre 10 days before the games opened.

1461
01:09:21.630 --> 01:09:23.940
There's a massacre at a big square in Mexico City,

1462
01:09:23.940 --> 01:09:25.770
where the army just takes out a bunch of students

1463
01:09:25.770 --> 01:09:26.733
who are protesting.

1464
01:09:27.900 --> 01:09:31.410
We don't know how many were killed in that,

1465
01:09:31.410 --> 01:09:33.423
but it could have been over 100 people.

1466
01:09:35.768 --> 01:09:37.320
It's just hard for us to wrap our minds

1467
01:09:37.320 --> 01:09:42.170
around how crazy that period was.

1468
01:09:43.230 --> 01:09:44.220
And here are these, and they're...

1469
01:09:44.220 --> 01:09:46.770
I don't use the word kids lightly,

1470
01:09:46.770 --> 01:09:50.883
but Evans and Smith and Carlos, they're 19 and 20 years old.

1471
01:09:52.350 --> 01:09:55.350
And they're suddenly thrust onto the world stage

1472
01:09:55.350 --> 01:09:57.300
in the middle of all of this maelstrom.

1473
01:09:58.560 --> 01:10:01.080
The whole thing just blows me away.

1474
01:10:01.080 --> 01:10:05.010
So Evans wears a beret as he's walking up onto the podium.

1475
01:10:05.010 --> 01:10:08.430
But then he takes it off off when they play the Anthem.

1476
01:10:08.430 --> 01:10:12.210
So that becomes a situation in which

1477
01:10:12.210 --> 01:10:14.550
certain people think that he went too far

1478
01:10:14.550 --> 01:10:17.160
and another group of people think that he didn't do enough.

1479
01:10:17.160 --> 01:10:22.160
So he's really pleasing nobody in this act in the moment.

1480
01:10:23.160 --> 01:10:26.010
And then has a really hard time for the rest of his life.

1481
01:10:26.010 --> 01:10:31.010
These guys can't get jobs, they really suffer.

1482
01:10:31.200 --> 01:10:34.143
They pay the price in a really material way.

1483
01:10:35.220 --> 01:10:40.220
No, the sacrifice is real in a way that it's...

1484
01:10:41.460 --> 01:10:44.970
It was just, you're angry black men, go away.

1485
01:10:44.970 --> 01:10:48.690
And there's a whole debate over what the fist meant,

1486
01:10:48.690 --> 01:10:49.860
did it mean black power?

1487
01:10:49.860 --> 01:10:51.660
Lee Evans was saying that I'm doing...

1488
01:10:51.660 --> 01:10:54.783
That was a Black Panther move, to wear the beret.

1489
01:10:56.120 --> 01:11:00.233
Whereas Tommy and John, they were trying to convey

1490
01:11:03.000 --> 01:11:05.760
this sense of solidarity with humanity

1491
01:11:05.760 --> 01:11:08.670
that got twisted and misinterpreted.

1492
01:11:08.670 --> 01:11:12.660
It's important to remember just what a kind of bogeyman

1493
01:11:12.660 --> 01:11:15.213
the Black Panther movement was in the late '60s.

1494
01:11:16.320 --> 01:11:19.770
Many white Americans were truly terrified,

1495
01:11:19.770 --> 01:11:23.490
of the notion that there would be a armed, radical,

1496
01:11:23.490 --> 01:11:28.490
violent part of the civil rights movement.

1497
01:11:30.330 --> 01:11:31.740
And that's what the Black Panthers were,

1498
01:11:31.740 --> 01:11:35.760
they were the radical arm of this fight for social justice.

1499
01:11:35.760 --> 01:11:38.230
And they did engage in acts of violence

1500
01:11:39.264 --> 01:11:42.360
and people were completely terrified of them.

1501
01:11:42.360 --> 01:11:43.830
So the idea that Lee Evans

1502
01:11:43.830 --> 01:11:46.990
would employ symbolism of the Black Panthers

1503
01:11:48.480 --> 01:11:51.123
as he made his way to the podium in the 400 meters,

1504
01:11:53.280 --> 01:11:54.830
it freaked a lot of people out.

1505
01:11:56.460 --> 01:11:59.190
It's interesting when you think about that

1506
01:11:59.190 --> 01:12:02.670
in juxtaposition with modern day versions of this.

1507
01:12:02.670 --> 01:12:05.520
Like I'm thinking of Steve Kerr's press conference

1508
01:12:05.520 --> 01:12:08.550
and how that really just seemed to resonate

1509
01:12:08.550 --> 01:12:10.653
across the board in a powerful way.

1510
01:12:12.390 --> 01:12:14.700
And he's a white man, so it's different qualitatively.

1511
01:12:14.700 --> 01:12:19.700
But does that happen without the legacy

1512
01:12:19.830 --> 01:12:22.413
of all these prior acts of courage?

1513
01:12:25.080 --> 01:12:26.760
I don't think it does.

1514
01:12:26.760 --> 01:12:29.160
Kerr's interesting and special cause his own father,

1515
01:12:29.160 --> 01:12:32.163
of course, was assassinated in a terrorist act.

1516
01:12:34.500 --> 01:12:39.500
And although my reaction to Kerr I always wonder

1517
01:12:39.720 --> 01:12:44.720
why so few NFL and NBA coaches don't follow his lead.

1518
01:12:47.100 --> 01:12:49.470
He does seem like he's still a little bit lonely out there

1519
01:12:49.470 --> 01:12:50.820
when he makes those stands.

1520
01:12:52.830 --> 01:12:56.460
Greg Popovich has made it clear that he belongs

1521
01:12:56.460 --> 01:12:58.230
on the same side as Steve Kerr.

1522
01:12:58.230 --> 01:13:03.220
But there's a long list of NBA coaches

1523
01:13:04.393 --> 01:13:07.977
who keep their mouth shut, I don't know why.

1524
01:13:07.977 --> 01:13:09.900
And it's interesting,

1525
01:13:09.900 --> 01:13:13.260
the differences between the various professional leagues,

1526
01:13:13.260 --> 01:13:15.870
like the NBA versus the NFL.

1527
01:13:15.870 --> 01:13:17.910
Which is a very different animal altogether

1528
01:13:17.910 --> 01:13:21.453
because of its customer base essentially.

1529
01:13:22.950 --> 01:13:24.543
No, the the NFL,

1530
01:13:26.730 --> 01:13:31.730
I always feel like the apocalypse could come,

1531
01:13:33.150 --> 01:13:35.370
global warming could wipe out all of humanity,

1532
01:13:35.370 --> 01:13:37.710
and the NFL will still be playing its games on Sunday.

1533
01:13:37.710 --> 01:13:41.610
I feel like (both laugh) there's something about them

1534
01:13:41.610 --> 01:13:43.680
that's never, ever gonna change.

1535
01:13:43.680 --> 01:13:48.300
And it's also interesting how the modern day athlete,

1536
01:13:48.300 --> 01:13:50.760
activist is showing up now,

1537
01:13:50.760 --> 01:13:53.220
it's mostly about mental health now

1538
01:13:53.220 --> 01:13:55.230
when these transgressive acts,

1539
01:13:55.230 --> 01:13:59.910
like you have Simone Biles and Naomi Osaka taking a stand

1540
01:13:59.910 --> 01:14:02.073
for their own personal wellbeing.

1541
01:14:03.030 --> 01:14:07.860
How do you think about that in relationship to the history?

1542
01:14:07.860 --> 01:14:10.020
Well, I have to say that

1543
01:14:10.020 --> 01:14:14.910
there was no controversy in recent Olympic history

1544
01:14:14.910 --> 01:14:19.650
that baffled me as much as the amount of hostility

1545
01:14:19.650 --> 01:14:22.560
that Simone Biles attracted

1546
01:14:22.560 --> 01:14:24.723
when she dropped out of that event.

1547
01:14:25.740 --> 01:14:27.720
To this day I don't get it.

1548
01:14:27.720 --> 01:14:32.040
Well I think when you think about the amateur ideal,

1549
01:14:32.040 --> 01:14:34.230
then it would be the purview of the athlete

1550
01:14:34.230 --> 01:14:36.360
to make that choice because they are an amateur.

1551
01:14:36.360 --> 01:14:40.140
But now it is so commercialized and comodified,

1552
01:14:40.140 --> 01:14:43.470
these people are a product and they're expected to perform.

1553
01:14:43.470 --> 01:14:47.370
And we as the audience are demanding of that.

1554
01:14:47.370 --> 01:14:50.220
But she's the greatest there ever was.

1555
01:14:50.220 --> 01:14:51.210
This is what I don't understand,

1556
01:14:51.210 --> 01:14:53.880
at what point shouldn't she earn?

1557
01:14:53.880 --> 01:14:57.900
So I don't understand anyone, for example,

1558
01:14:57.900 --> 01:14:59.940
who would feel they have standing

1559
01:14:59.940 --> 01:15:03.390
to criticize a decision she makes about her own performance.

1560
01:15:03.390 --> 01:15:05.373
She's the best there ever was.

1561
01:15:08.569 --> 01:15:10.500
It would be like me criticizing Michael Jordan

1562
01:15:10.500 --> 01:15:12.933
over the way he shot a jump shot.

1563
01:15:14.370 --> 01:15:16.420
He's Michael Jordan, you can't criticize.

1564
01:15:18.270 --> 01:15:19.530
You're not qualified to make that jump shot.

1565
01:15:19.530 --> 01:15:20.640
She's the GOAT,

1566
01:15:20.640 --> 01:15:24.300
and she's doing something which is not a...

1567
01:15:24.300 --> 01:15:26.583
She's engaged in a sport which is nuts.

1568
01:15:27.660 --> 01:15:31.830
It's like the most dangerous high wire act

1569
01:15:31.830 --> 01:15:33.570
and she's doing stuff that no one

1570
01:15:33.570 --> 01:15:37.590
in the history of the world has ever done in gymnastics.

1571
01:15:37.590 --> 01:15:42.510
She makes a call, she has an experience that shakes her

1572
01:15:42.510 --> 01:15:45.930
and shakes her, this is the toughest athlete

1573
01:15:45.930 --> 01:15:48.900
that ever walked into a gymnastics arena.

1574
01:15:48.900 --> 01:15:52.920
And she says, "It's in my own best interest not to go on."

1575
01:15:52.920 --> 01:15:57.660
I would have thought that we would all have just sat there

1576
01:15:57.660 --> 01:16:02.660
and said, "Oh my God, tell us more, what can we learn?"

1577
01:16:04.080 --> 01:16:05.370
And then instead, what she gets

1578
01:16:05.370 --> 01:16:07.650
is this tidal wave of criticism

1579
01:16:07.650 --> 01:16:09.990
from people who probably haven't even strapped down

1580
01:16:09.990 --> 01:16:12.240
a pair of running shoes in the last 15 years.

1581
01:16:14.400 --> 01:16:17.100
I was like, this was one of the lowest moments

1582
01:16:17.100 --> 01:16:20.880
in American public discourse in a very long time.

1583
01:16:20.880 --> 01:16:21.930
So what is that?

1584
01:16:21.930 --> 01:16:24.480
Is it this sense, this archaic sense

1585
01:16:24.480 --> 01:16:27.060
that if you're a true competitor,

1586
01:16:27.060 --> 01:16:29.970
you don't opt out of difficult scenarios?

1587
01:16:29.970 --> 01:16:33.153
What is the antecedent of that?

1588
01:16:34.440 --> 01:16:37.923
Part of this is this idea, and it goes back to Bud Winter,

1589
01:16:39.630 --> 01:16:43.080
Bud Winter, when he reinvents sprinting,

1590
01:16:43.080 --> 01:16:45.840
he's confronting a kind of intuitive notion

1591
01:16:45.840 --> 01:16:48.033
about what effort looks like.

1592
01:16:48.960 --> 01:16:52.290
And his point is that our intuitive notion,

1593
01:16:52.290 --> 01:16:54.120
that in order to get the most

1594
01:16:54.120 --> 01:16:58.020
out of any kind of physical exertion,

1595
01:16:58.020 --> 01:16:59.973
you have to display obvious effort,

1596
01:17:02.010 --> 01:17:03.840
he said that notion is false,

1597
01:17:03.840 --> 01:17:07.950
that paradoxically, the best way to run

1598
01:17:07.950 --> 01:17:09.850
as fast as you can possibly run

1599
01:17:14.143 --> 01:17:16.200
is not to look like you're running as fast

1600
01:17:16.200 --> 01:17:19.290
and not to act as if you're trying to run as fast as you...

1601
01:17:19.290 --> 01:17:22.053
That through relaxation, comes peak performance.

1602
01:17:23.130 --> 01:17:25.680
The root of that intuitive notion,

1603
01:17:25.680 --> 01:17:27.600
that you need to look like you're...

1604
01:17:27.600 --> 01:17:30.180
The thing that he was trying to confront

1605
01:17:30.180 --> 01:17:34.320
is this idea that you can grit and grimace your way

1606
01:17:34.320 --> 01:17:39.320
through the perseverance even in the face of of pain

1607
01:17:41.940 --> 01:17:44.970
or difficulty is always the best course,

1608
01:17:44.970 --> 01:17:48.600
that that's what a hero is, the person who perseveres.

1609
01:17:48.600 --> 01:17:52.443
And we know real athletes know that to be false.

1610
01:17:54.114 --> 01:17:54.947
If you get an injury,

1611
01:17:54.947 --> 01:17:59.280
the first thing you should do is stop not persevere.

1612
01:17:59.280 --> 01:18:00.960
Perseverance is sometimes useful,

1613
01:18:00.960 --> 01:18:02.850
but elite athletes know the difference

1614
01:18:02.850 --> 01:18:05.043
between wise and unwise perseverance.

1615
01:18:06.150 --> 01:18:08.050
I think that there's a class of people

1616
01:18:09.387 --> 01:18:11.337
who didn't appreciate that distinction.

1617
01:18:12.720 --> 01:18:15.000
No one has persevered more than Simone Biles,

1618
01:18:15.000 --> 01:18:20.000
but she perseveres wisely, she doesn't persevere unwisely.

1619
01:18:21.750 --> 01:18:26.750
There's a troglodyte, pre-21st century, pre-20th century

1620
01:18:27.360 --> 01:18:30.720
notion that doesn't make that crucial distinction.

1621
01:18:30.720 --> 01:18:33.603
There's also, I think, a sense of ownership.

1622
01:18:34.770 --> 01:18:37.140
Like we have, you talk about standing,

1623
01:18:37.140 --> 01:18:41.550
but have this expectation and you need to jump

1624
01:18:41.550 --> 01:18:43.530
when we say jump.

1625
01:18:43.530 --> 01:18:45.840
A strange relationship

1626
01:18:45.840 --> 01:18:49.323
between audience and athlete that is perverse.

1627
01:18:50.400 --> 01:18:52.683
Yeah, there is that is interesting.

1628
01:18:54.870 --> 01:18:57.810
That the fan, by virtue of their fandom,

1629
01:18:57.810 --> 01:19:01.307
feels they have some ownership of the athlete.

1630
01:19:06.330 --> 01:19:08.130
But it's also a weird idea.

1631
01:19:08.130 --> 01:19:09.663
I know, I know.

1632
01:19:11.130 --> 01:19:12.270
The other thing about Bud Winter,

1633
01:19:12.270 --> 01:19:15.730
is he's also doing visualization right?

1634
01:19:15.730 --> 01:19:16.950
Yeah.
That's key

1635
01:19:16.950 --> 01:19:21.950
to any athlete now that's part and parcel of the ABCs

1636
01:19:22.350 --> 01:19:25.920
of preparing for your sport.

1637
01:19:25.920 --> 01:19:28.740
But that was revolutionary at the time too,

1638
01:19:28.740 --> 01:19:30.870
this idea of can you be present?

1639
01:19:30.870 --> 01:19:32.580
Can you walk through...

1640
01:19:32.580 --> 01:19:35.343
The whole mental game begins with him.

1641
01:19:37.110 --> 01:19:38.700
And he teaches it so powerfully

1642
01:19:38.700 --> 01:19:42.630
that he got a guy with a groin pull to set a world record

1643
01:19:42.630 --> 01:19:43.980
in 200 meters (both laugh).

1644
01:19:45.332 --> 01:19:49.290
I'd one groin problem in my life, it was so terrible.

1645
01:19:49.290 --> 01:19:51.420
And it takes like nine months for it to heal, right?

1646
01:19:51.420 --> 01:19:54.107
Exactly, that is right (laughs).

1647
01:19:54.107 --> 01:19:57.810
And he just takes him out on the track and calms him down

1648
01:19:57.810 --> 01:20:02.810
and they do some 60% efforts, some 80% efforts,

1649
01:20:03.150 --> 01:20:04.920
gets his mind right.

1650
01:20:04.920 --> 01:20:07.110
And then it's so beautiful

1651
01:20:07.110 --> 01:20:11.130
how Tommy walks through second by second

1652
01:20:11.130 --> 01:20:12.300
him being on the starting blocks,

1653
01:20:12.300 --> 01:20:15.450
not being able to warm up his starts like everybody else,

1654
01:20:15.450 --> 01:20:16.920
not knowing what's gonna happen.

1655
01:20:16.920 --> 01:20:21.920
But when the gun goes off, all the training comes into play

1656
01:20:22.020 --> 01:20:25.860
and his aperture just narrows down

1657
01:20:25.860 --> 01:20:28.980
to what he knows how to do through-

1658
01:20:28.980 --> 01:20:30.300
But it's really interesting

1659
01:20:30.300 --> 01:20:32.430
because his injury forces him to do

1660
01:20:32.430 --> 01:20:34.530
the very thing that Winter's trying to do,

1661
01:20:34.530 --> 01:20:39.510
which is the best way to run it 100% is not to run it 100%.

1662
01:20:39.510 --> 01:20:42.360
So he's holding himself back

1663
01:20:42.360 --> 01:20:44.610
and the result of just a little bit,

1664
01:20:44.610 --> 01:20:46.380
he won't go right to the...

1665
01:20:46.380 --> 01:20:48.660
Which would be the danger in the Olympic final,

1666
01:20:48.660 --> 01:20:51.060
is that you do push yourself too far,

1667
01:20:51.060 --> 01:20:53.280
particularly in a sport like sprinting.

1668
01:20:53.280 --> 01:20:54.930
So he can't do that.

1669
01:20:54.930 --> 01:20:57.450
So in a weird way, maybe he's freed up

1670
01:20:57.450 --> 01:20:59.693
to have the greatest performance of his life.

1671
01:21:00.750 --> 01:21:03.120
Which is an interesting thing with running in general.

1672
01:21:03.120 --> 01:21:08.120
Because so much of running is about holding back, economy.

1673
01:21:08.580 --> 01:21:11.610
So this is funny, I once had a conversation,

1674
01:21:11.610 --> 01:21:16.380
I won't use his name, with a Nobel prize winning economist

1675
01:21:16.380 --> 01:21:19.680
who you've probably heard of, who I genuinely like,

1676
01:21:19.680 --> 01:21:20.513
I've done it for a long time.

1677
01:21:20.513 --> 01:21:23.043
He's a brilliant lovely guy, not an athlete.

1678
01:21:24.125 --> 01:21:27.390
And he said, we were talking about running

1679
01:21:27.390 --> 01:21:30.030
and we're talking about middle distance running.

1680
01:21:30.030 --> 01:21:34.357
He was like he found it all baffling, he said,

1681
01:21:34.357 --> 01:21:37.050
"I don't understand why you just don't go out

1682
01:21:37.050 --> 01:21:39.807
and run as hard as you can for as long as you can."

1683
01:21:39.807 --> 01:21:41.857
And he thought like...

1684
01:21:41.857 --> 01:21:46.013
And I was incapable of explaining to him why that was wrong,

1685
01:21:47.340 --> 01:21:48.790
I couldn't do it, I couldn't.

1686
01:21:50.250 --> 01:21:52.650
Imagine a guy who's never exercised a day in his life

1687
01:21:52.650 --> 01:21:57.300
but has an IQ of 200, he's a purely rational.

1688
01:21:57.300 --> 01:22:00.660
He was like, "Well you have X amount of energy.

1689
01:22:00.660 --> 01:22:04.590
Surely the danger is that, you have 100 points of energy,

1690
01:22:04.590 --> 01:22:06.360
the danger is you'll finish the race

1691
01:22:06.360 --> 01:22:07.810
and you'll only have used 90.

1692
01:22:08.700 --> 01:22:11.090
So the best way to ensure you use all 100

1693
01:22:11.090 --> 01:22:13.890
is just to go as hard as you can for as long as you can.

1694
01:22:14.910 --> 01:22:18.030
But that's the same mistake you can make with F1.

1695
01:22:18.030 --> 01:22:19.500
Well, just get out there

1696
01:22:19.500 --> 01:22:21.060
and drive faster than the other guy.

1697
01:22:21.060 --> 01:22:23.610
And then you learn, oh my God, this is so complex

1698
01:22:23.610 --> 01:22:24.870
and there's so much strategy here

1699
01:22:24.870 --> 01:22:26.727
and the tires and all the stuff,

1700
01:22:26.727 --> 01:22:29.283
and you realize it's not about that at all.

1701
01:22:33.578 --> 01:22:35.760
The great privilege of being a journalist

1702
01:22:35.760 --> 01:22:39.450
is that you learn, in the best possible way,

1703
01:22:39.450 --> 01:22:44.450
how large gap is between lay understanding of some field

1704
01:22:44.910 --> 01:22:46.263
and expert understanding.

1705
01:22:50.100 --> 01:22:53.160
If you're a layperson under normal circumstances,

1706
01:22:53.160 --> 01:22:56.160
I think you think that gap is smaller than it actually is.

1707
01:22:56.160 --> 01:22:57.363
You think, yeah,

1708
01:22:58.830 --> 01:23:01.620
my doctor knows more about medicine than I do.

1709
01:23:01.620 --> 01:23:03.240
But I did a Google search before I came,

1710
01:23:03.240 --> 01:23:05.277
and I'm pretty sure what I have is this

1711
01:23:05.277 --> 01:23:06.810
and doctor says I don't.

1712
01:23:06.810 --> 01:23:07.860
And like, "Are you sure doctor?"

1713
01:23:07.860 --> 01:23:10.020
We think it's a narrow difference between...

1714
01:23:10.020 --> 01:23:12.810
When you're a journalist and you report stories,

1715
01:23:12.810 --> 01:23:14.760
what you get is a reminder

1716
01:23:14.760 --> 01:23:18.030
every time you pick up the phone and do an interview,

1717
01:23:18.030 --> 01:23:20.970
that your knowledge is way down here

1718
01:23:20.970 --> 01:23:23.817
and the person you're talking to's knowledge is way up here.

1719
01:23:23.817 --> 01:23:27.150
And that gap is 10 times larger than you thought it was

1720
01:23:27.150 --> 01:23:28.600
when you picked up the phone.

1721
01:23:30.720 --> 01:23:33.480
That's the great gift of journalism, it is an act,

1722
01:23:33.480 --> 01:23:38.480
it is a discipline that reinforces humility at every turn.

1723
01:23:40.140 --> 01:23:43.020
What's the theory, the Dunning Kruger effect,

1724
01:23:43.020 --> 01:23:43.890
where the less you know,

1725
01:23:43.890 --> 01:23:46.920
the more you think you have command over terrain.

1726
01:23:46.920 --> 01:23:49.440
Journalism is reversed Dunning Kruger.

1727
01:23:49.440 --> 01:23:51.033
Yeah, good for the soul.

1728
01:23:54.330 --> 01:23:56.220
When your curiosity is piqued

1729
01:23:56.220 --> 01:23:58.560
and you think, this is something I wanna learn more about

1730
01:23:58.560 --> 01:24:00.633
and you go into that interviewer mode,

1731
01:24:01.500 --> 01:24:06.500
what is your strategy or super-power as an interviewer

1732
01:24:07.440 --> 01:24:10.230
to get the best out of the person that you're talking to?

1733
01:24:10.230 --> 01:24:11.853
Ask dumb questions.

1734
01:24:12.840 --> 01:24:14.460
Which is an act of humility, right?

1735
01:24:14.460 --> 01:24:16.350
Yes, yes.

1736
01:24:16.350 --> 01:24:19.050
Because you'll discover that very often

1737
01:24:19.050 --> 01:24:21.750
what you thought was a dumb question's not dumb at all

1738
01:24:23.670 --> 01:24:26.700
and that the knowledge you presume you already have,

1739
01:24:26.700 --> 01:24:28.203
you probably don't have.

1740
01:24:29.400 --> 01:24:31.800
I try not to overs script interviews

1741
01:24:31.800 --> 01:24:34.080
because the most value in an interview

1742
01:24:34.080 --> 01:24:36.150
comes from the unexpected places

1743
01:24:36.150 --> 01:24:38.100
the person that you're talking to goes.

1744
01:24:39.480 --> 01:24:41.280
Which again, is a version of the same thing,

1745
01:24:41.280 --> 01:24:42.633
don't pretend you know.

1746
01:24:43.530 --> 01:24:44.750
Sometimes I interview people

1747
01:24:44.750 --> 01:24:48.540
or I see people who are preparing for an interview

1748
01:24:48.540 --> 01:24:52.683
and they have 25 questions written out and I think-

1749
01:24:53.988 --> 01:24:55.738
I'm closing my outline right now.

1750
01:24:57.630 --> 01:24:59.073
Which is just my security blanket anyway.

1751
01:24:59.073 --> 01:25:00.387
No, it's useful, it's useful, it's useful.

1752
01:25:00.387 --> 01:25:04.200
But I always say, are you really so sure

1753
01:25:04.200 --> 01:25:06.600
that you know the direction the conversation's gonna go?

1754
01:25:06.600 --> 01:25:09.813
And so, are you sure you want to direct the conversation?

1755
01:25:11.580 --> 01:25:12.990
Whatever happened to listening

1756
01:25:12.990 --> 01:25:15.200
and to responding and improv?

1757
01:25:17.564 --> 01:25:19.170
The best interviews

1758
01:25:19.170 --> 01:25:21.510
have an element of improvisation in them.

1759
01:25:21.510 --> 01:25:25.530
And I'm thinking of some of the ones I did

1760
01:25:25.530 --> 01:25:27.720
for this season of "Revisionist History"

1761
01:25:27.720 --> 01:25:32.040
where there was some kind of surprising turn,

1762
01:25:32.040 --> 01:25:34.110
often it's with emotion,

1763
01:25:34.110 --> 01:25:37.170
that you won't understand the parts of the story

1764
01:25:37.170 --> 01:25:39.120
that you're eliciting from someone,

1765
01:25:39.120 --> 01:25:42.510
that have a great emotional meaning to them.

1766
01:25:42.510 --> 01:25:44.820
You've have no way of knowing.

1767
01:25:44.820 --> 01:25:49.820
And then when that happens, you need to be prepared to pause

1768
01:25:49.920 --> 01:25:52.430
and reflect on that emotion as opposed to...

1769
01:25:54.030 --> 01:25:57.180
The rookie era in interviewing is that

1770
01:25:57.180 --> 01:25:59.913
people are so in a hurry to get to the next question

1771
01:25:59.913 --> 01:26:00.960
that they don't-

1772
01:26:00.960 --> 01:26:01.793
Of course.

1773
01:26:02.880 --> 01:26:03.713
Pause.

1774
01:26:03.713 --> 01:26:04.860
Of course.

1775
01:26:04.860 --> 01:26:06.870
We've both been guests on podcasts

1776
01:26:06.870 --> 01:26:09.690
where somebody has their list.

1777
01:26:09.690 --> 01:26:11.490
They're not listening to what you say.

1778
01:26:11.490 --> 01:26:13.860
You answer the question, they say awesome,

1779
01:26:13.860 --> 01:26:14.940
and then they ask the next,

1780
01:26:14.940 --> 01:26:17.100
they're thinking about the next question

1781
01:26:17.100 --> 01:26:20.040
The problem that I always run into is,

1782
01:26:20.040 --> 01:26:22.713
obviously I talk to people and this is what I do,

1783
01:26:24.660 --> 01:26:27.120
when it's somebody like yourself

1784
01:26:27.120 --> 01:26:28.440
that I'm really excited to talk to,

1785
01:26:28.440 --> 01:26:30.660
I do have a tendency to over prepare

1786
01:26:30.660 --> 01:26:33.120
'cause wanna be on top of everything.

1787
01:26:33.120 --> 01:26:37.590
And it does rob the experience of any surprise.

1788
01:26:37.590 --> 01:26:42.590
If I already know too much, then it boxes you into a corner

1789
01:26:42.720 --> 01:26:44.880
and where do you go from that?

1790
01:26:44.880 --> 01:26:48.270
But it takes a level of security to hold yourself back.

1791
01:26:48.270 --> 01:26:50.580
Like in running, I'm gonna hold myself back.

1792
01:26:50.580 --> 01:26:52.200
I know I could read all this other stuff,

1793
01:26:52.200 --> 01:26:54.240
but I'm not gonna do that.

1794
01:26:54.240 --> 01:26:57.540
Do I have enough confidence in my conversational ability

1795
01:26:57.540 --> 01:27:00.480
to just show up and be present and listen?

1796
01:27:00.480 --> 01:27:01.440
You go to a dinner party,

1797
01:27:01.440 --> 01:27:02.790
you don't prepare for a dinner party,

1798
01:27:02.790 --> 01:27:04.560
you go and you engage with the person.

1799
01:27:04.560 --> 01:27:06.900
Can we have that type of experience and trust

1800
01:27:06.900 --> 01:27:10.170
that it will go in the direction that it needs to go?

1801
01:27:10.170 --> 01:27:14.043
Well, but I don't want you to be too hard on yourself.

1802
01:27:15.110 --> 01:27:15.943
if I'm-

1803
01:27:15.943 --> 01:27:17.400
But if I'm doing it,

1804
01:27:17.400 --> 01:27:19.500
I'm gonna obviously listen to the podcast

1805
01:27:19.500 --> 01:27:21.750
and understand the terrain and the subject matter.

1806
01:27:21.750 --> 01:27:25.500
It would be disrespectful to you,

1807
01:27:25.500 --> 01:27:27.630
who's going out of your way to come here and spend time,

1808
01:27:27.630 --> 01:27:28.893
to not do that.

1809
01:27:30.900 --> 01:27:32.700
The way I would phrase it is that,

1810
01:27:37.860 --> 01:27:39.270
the best kind of preparation

1811
01:27:39.270 --> 01:27:42.360
is it simply allows you to understand

1812
01:27:42.360 --> 01:27:43.530
all the things that you don't know.

1813
01:27:43.530 --> 01:27:46.920
So for example, if I'm talking to some scientist

1814
01:27:46.920 --> 01:27:48.300
who's written a research paper,

1815
01:27:48.300 --> 01:27:50.790
I would totally read the research paper first,

1816
01:27:50.790 --> 01:27:53.640
but that's only to generate a whole series of questions.

1817
01:27:53.640 --> 01:27:57.000
Invariably, deep reading of anyone's work

1818
01:27:57.000 --> 01:27:59.520
usually gives you an insight

1819
01:27:59.520 --> 01:28:02.834
into all the things that you don't know,

1820
01:28:02.834 --> 01:28:05.280
the further things, the things that were left unsaid,

1821
01:28:05.280 --> 01:28:07.263
the assumptions that weren't.

1822
01:28:08.888 --> 01:28:12.180
People's research or people's work

1823
01:28:12.180 --> 01:28:15.330
is only an approximation of their knowledge,

1824
01:28:15.330 --> 01:28:17.040
it's a little window.

1825
01:28:17.040 --> 01:28:20.490
So if I know, oh okay, I can see this little piece of it,

1826
01:28:20.490 --> 01:28:22.770
then I know oh, I need to ask questions

1827
01:28:22.770 --> 01:28:26.756
about all of the hidden underpinnings.

1828
01:28:26.756 --> 01:28:27.987
And then you take all of that

1829
01:28:27.987 --> 01:28:32.910
and you do your Gladwellian synthesis, like only you can do,

1830
01:28:32.910 --> 01:28:36.090
to create these works, whether written or audio.

1831
01:28:36.090 --> 01:28:39.810
And I wanted to talk a little bit about the advent,

1832
01:28:39.810 --> 01:28:42.810
like this explosion in creative audio

1833
01:28:42.810 --> 01:28:44.040
that you're at the center of.

1834
01:28:44.040 --> 01:28:45.390
I think it's so interesting,

1835
01:28:45.390 --> 01:28:50.390
especially in context to legacy publishing,

1836
01:28:50.820 --> 01:28:53.790
what's broken about that, where they're myopic

1837
01:28:53.790 --> 01:28:57.930
about where modern audiences place their attention.

1838
01:28:57.930 --> 01:29:02.723
Well, I got interested in rethinking audio because

1839
01:29:05.160 --> 01:29:08.700
when I got to New York 20 some odd years ago,

1840
01:29:08.700 --> 01:29:09.533
I'd ride the subway

1841
01:29:09.533 --> 01:29:11.490
and everyone would be reading magazines and books.

1842
01:29:11.490 --> 01:29:15.510
And then five years ago, get in a subway

1843
01:29:15.510 --> 01:29:18.090
and everyone was listening to something with their ears.

1844
01:29:18.090 --> 01:29:23.090
And I realized, oh, I've just lost an audience.

1845
01:29:24.930 --> 01:29:28.560
And if I don't move from the eyes to the ears,

1846
01:29:28.560 --> 01:29:30.060
I'm out of a job.

1847
01:29:30.060 --> 01:29:31.510
Or at least not out of a job,

1848
01:29:34.170 --> 01:29:36.563
there's a whole group of people that I'm just gonna miss.

1849
01:29:37.710 --> 01:29:39.510
Then my question was, well, why are the things

1850
01:29:39.510 --> 01:29:41.973
that we're giving them to listen to so bad?

1851
01:29:43.200 --> 01:29:45.030
You write a book and you just read the book

1852
01:29:45.030 --> 01:29:47.940
into a microphone in a closet.
Horrible.

1853
01:29:47.940 --> 01:29:49.286
What's that?
It's horrible

1854
01:29:49.286 --> 01:29:50.520
It's horrible.
Most of those experiences

1855
01:29:50.520 --> 01:29:51.447
are terrible.
They're terrible.

1856
01:29:51.447 --> 01:29:52.980
And they're generally worse

1857
01:29:52.980 --> 01:29:57.363
with some overly trained voice actor reading them.

1858
01:29:58.710 --> 01:30:00.270
It is amazing.

1859
01:30:00.270 --> 01:30:04.260
So we decided let's do real stuff,

1860
01:30:04.260 --> 01:30:06.180
stuff that's worthy of being listened to.

1861
01:30:06.180 --> 01:30:08.617
So it can be as simple as, in my book

1862
01:30:08.617 --> 01:30:11.943
"Talking to Strangers," it was like a podcast.

1863
01:30:13.020 --> 01:30:16.020
All of the interviews I did, I have tape,

1864
01:30:16.020 --> 01:30:18.900
and I used that tape, and I used archival tape.

1865
01:30:18.900 --> 01:30:20.340
If I'm talking about general so-and-so,

1866
01:30:20.340 --> 01:30:21.360
you hear general so-and-so,

1867
01:30:21.360 --> 01:30:23.403
you don't hear me saying he said this.

1868
01:30:24.540 --> 01:30:28.500
When I'm talking about some special bomber,

1869
01:30:28.500 --> 01:30:29.610
you're gonna hear the bomber.

1870
01:30:29.610 --> 01:30:30.660
We did that whole...

1871
01:30:30.660 --> 01:30:32.250
It's not just a matter of taking

1872
01:30:32.250 --> 01:30:35.939
what would be appropriate in book form

1873
01:30:35.939 --> 01:30:37.980
and just vocalizing it.

1874
01:30:37.980 --> 01:30:42.450
These are different mediums that have different priorities

1875
01:30:42.450 --> 01:30:45.210
in terms of how you bring the best

1876
01:30:45.210 --> 01:30:47.670
out of this story that you're trying to tell.

1877
01:30:47.670 --> 01:30:49.160
They have very different...

1878
01:30:52.500 --> 01:30:55.740
Audio is so much more emotional that it changes,

1879
01:30:55.740 --> 01:30:58.620
if you're gonna think in terms of telling a story in audio,

1880
01:30:58.620 --> 01:31:01.800
it changes the kind of story that you can tell.

1881
01:31:01.800 --> 01:31:04.290
So when I think about podcast episodes,

1882
01:31:04.290 --> 01:31:07.230
the best podcast episodes I do in "Revisionist History"

1883
01:31:07.230 --> 01:31:10.770
are episodes that could not be done in print.

1884
01:31:10.770 --> 01:31:14.283
Just the story wouldn't work, wouldn't be interesting,

1885
01:31:15.660 --> 01:31:16.773
and vice versa.

1886
01:31:17.610 --> 01:31:19.110
Now, there are some that can work

1887
01:31:19.110 --> 01:31:23.250
but they tend to be B-level podcast episodes.

1888
01:31:23.250 --> 01:31:27.940
The great ones, like I did it one a couple years ago

1889
01:31:29.176 --> 01:31:32.460
on Elvis, there's a song that Elvis sang.

1890
01:31:32.460 --> 01:31:34.617
And he would always botch the lyrics of it.

1891
01:31:34.617 --> 01:31:36.496
The one with Jack White.

1892
01:31:36.496 --> 01:31:37.860
Yeah, yeah, yeah, with Jack White.

1893
01:31:37.860 --> 01:31:40.010
That's one, you can write that,

1894
01:31:40.010 --> 01:31:41.850
it would be interesting kind of.

1895
01:31:41.850 --> 01:31:44.250
You had to hear it, you gotta hear Elvis,

1896
01:31:44.250 --> 01:31:45.083
you gotta hear Jack.

1897
01:31:45.083 --> 01:31:46.530
I'm in the studio with Jack White,

1898
01:31:46.530 --> 01:31:49.770
there's this woman named Casey Bowls,

1899
01:31:49.770 --> 01:31:51.510
who sings a song in the middle of that,

1900
01:31:51.510 --> 01:31:53.610
where she breaks down in the middle of the thing,

1901
01:31:53.610 --> 01:31:55.565
everyone's crying.

1902
01:31:55.565 --> 01:31:56.850
It was just this nuts thing.

1903
01:31:56.850 --> 01:31:59.850
But you have to hear it, it doesn't work on the page,

1904
01:31:59.850 --> 01:32:00.930
that's the gold standard.

1905
01:32:00.930 --> 01:32:04.110
Yeah, very difficult to achieve that.

1906
01:32:04.110 --> 01:32:06.720
I think people underappreciate how hard it is

1907
01:32:06.720 --> 01:32:07.680
to do what you do.

1908
01:32:07.680 --> 01:32:11.460
You're essentially making documentaries without cameras

1909
01:32:11.460 --> 01:32:14.550
You need a team of incredibly skilled people.

1910
01:32:14.550 --> 01:32:18.570
I'm sure it's unbelievably time intensive and expensive

1911
01:32:18.570 --> 01:32:21.690
and very different from the writing process

1912
01:32:21.690 --> 01:32:23.520
of you going out, interviewing people,

1913
01:32:23.520 --> 01:32:25.890
and sitting at your desk and writing something,

1914
01:32:25.890 --> 01:32:27.240
this is a team effort, right?

1915
01:32:27.240 --> 01:32:30.420
So suddenly you have to be a manager of people

1916
01:32:30.420 --> 01:32:31.980
and you have to be in meetings.

1917
01:32:31.980 --> 01:32:35.490
And it's a whole different personality trait

1918
01:32:35.490 --> 01:32:36.987
that you have to leverage to make these things.

1919
01:32:36.987 --> 01:32:39.633
It's also really fun, it's really fun.

1920
01:32:40.751 --> 01:32:42.157
I heard you say something like,

1921
01:32:42.157 --> 01:32:43.390
"Now you have to be in all these meetings and you're like,

1922
01:32:43.390 --> 01:32:46.760
'This is fantastic, I get to talk to people.'"

1923
01:32:48.870 --> 01:32:49.860
Most writers are like

1924
01:32:49.860 --> 01:32:52.710
they've completely constructed their lives to avoid that.

1925
01:32:54.631 --> 01:32:56.873
I have a great team and we have a lot of fun.

1926
01:32:58.180 --> 01:33:00.990
And every year, we try and do a reporting trip,

1927
01:33:00.990 --> 01:33:03.303
I take two of my producers.

1928
01:33:04.320 --> 01:33:07.200
Last year, we went, cause I did an episode on these dogs

1929
01:33:07.200 --> 01:33:08.450
that can sniff for COVID,

1930
01:33:09.330 --> 01:33:14.330
we went to this hills in Alabama, we had such a blast.

1931
01:33:14.970 --> 01:33:18.030
And then this year, we have an episode

1932
01:33:18.030 --> 01:33:23.030
that's all about the woman, Margaret Mitchell,

1933
01:33:24.390 --> 01:33:25.830
who wrote "Gone with the Wind,"

1934
01:33:25.830 --> 01:33:27.630
was killed in a drunk driving crash.

1935
01:33:29.100 --> 01:33:30.810
So we went to Atlanta.

1936
01:33:30.810 --> 01:33:32.430
All these trips end up going to Atlanta

1937
01:33:32.430 --> 01:33:33.540
'cause I love Atlanta.

1938
01:33:33.540 --> 01:33:35.010
But we ended up gonna her...

1939
01:33:35.010 --> 01:33:38.730
All of her papers where in Atlanta.

1940
01:33:38.730 --> 01:33:41.463
Then we went to the second half of the story,

1941
01:33:41.463 --> 01:33:43.380
it's too hard to explain now,

1942
01:33:43.380 --> 01:33:46.650
but it's all about the original "A Star Is Born" movie.

1943
01:33:46.650 --> 01:33:47.730
And the papers of that

1944
01:33:47.730 --> 01:33:49.950
are at the University of Georgia in Athens.

1945
01:33:49.950 --> 01:33:51.420
So we did this really fun road trip,

1946
01:33:51.420 --> 01:33:54.570
where we were wandering around Atlanta

1947
01:33:54.570 --> 01:33:56.970
recreating the crash scene and gonna Athens

1948
01:33:56.970 --> 01:34:01.083
and reading through hundred-year-old files.

1949
01:34:02.250 --> 01:34:03.543
We enjoy ourselves.

1950
01:34:04.380 --> 01:34:06.180
It just seems like such a heavy lift.

1951
01:34:06.180 --> 01:34:09.810
I just know I do the simplest version of this possible,

1952
01:34:09.810 --> 01:34:11.910
and I know how much it consumes my life.

1953
01:34:11.910 --> 01:34:14.340
To hear that, you go out, you do all that.

1954
01:34:14.340 --> 01:34:16.410
You have to bring it back, you gotta listen to all of it,

1955
01:34:16.410 --> 01:34:18.810
you gotta figure out what is the narrative here.

1956
01:34:22.066 --> 01:34:24.330
That's a challenge I would imagine

1957
01:34:24.330 --> 01:34:27.120
I won't be able to do it for ever.

1958
01:34:27.120 --> 01:34:29.913
Although I don't mean to, it's fun.

1959
01:34:31.050 --> 01:34:34.953
Like all things, it gets much easier the longer you do it.

1960
01:34:36.750 --> 01:34:39.060
And remember, before I started podcasting,

1961
01:34:39.060 --> 01:34:41.460
I had two previous careers.

1962
01:34:41.460 --> 01:34:44.460
One is a newspaper reporter, one is a magazine writer,

1963
01:34:44.460 --> 01:34:47.160
and they were enormously...

1964
01:34:47.160 --> 01:34:49.380
That kind of preparation is incredibly useful.

1965
01:34:49.380 --> 01:34:53.850
So the thing you learn, I was 20 years a newspaper reporter,

1966
01:34:53.850 --> 01:34:56.250
and what you learn in being a newspaper reporter

1967
01:34:56.250 --> 01:34:59.130
is efficiency, it's all about efficiency.

1968
01:34:59.130 --> 01:35:01.920
So it's about how quickly can I master a subject?

1969
01:35:01.920 --> 01:35:03.480
How quickly can I spot a story?

1970
01:35:03.480 --> 01:35:06.393
How quickly can I represent the story?

1971
01:35:10.590 --> 01:35:14.250
That was a 20 year long masterclass,

1972
01:35:14.250 --> 01:35:17.730
and I'm still living off those lessons I learned.

1973
01:35:17.730 --> 01:35:19.830
Where there's no room for writer's block.

1974
01:35:19.830 --> 01:35:20.663
No.

1975
01:35:20.663 --> 01:35:22.710
You're just bowling forward at all times

1976
01:35:22.710 --> 01:35:26.910
And that idea of zeroing in very quickly

1977
01:35:26.910 --> 01:35:29.490
on where you think the gold is,

1978
01:35:29.490 --> 01:35:30.940
that's what you learn as a...

1979
01:35:33.750 --> 01:35:36.360
I don't even know how you would learn it any other way.

1980
01:35:36.360 --> 01:35:38.523
But you're getting so many reps.

1981
01:35:39.720 --> 01:35:43.653
You might write four, five, six stories a week for 20 years.

1982
01:35:44.670 --> 01:35:46.845
Actually I wasn't a newspaper reporter for 20 years,

1983
01:35:46.845 --> 01:35:49.623
I was newspaper reporter for 10, I always doubled it.

1984
01:35:51.115 --> 01:35:52.676
It was 10 years, 10 years.

1985
01:35:52.676 --> 01:35:54.150
Is that a senior moment?

1986
01:35:54.150 --> 01:35:57.270
Well, I'm conflating it with my magazine stuff,

1987
01:35:57.270 --> 01:36:00.120
it was 20 years of newspaper and magazine.

1988
01:36:00.120 --> 01:36:02.250
So how does that leave you?

1989
01:36:02.250 --> 01:36:05.010
I know "Bomber Mafia" was audiobook first.

1990
01:36:05.010 --> 01:36:08.223
So how do you think about print media now?

1991
01:36:09.210 --> 01:36:12.750
When you consider that you can reach so many people

1992
01:36:12.750 --> 01:36:14.010
through the audio products

1993
01:36:14.010 --> 01:36:16.080
and you can do this fun creative stuff

1994
01:36:16.080 --> 01:36:18.480
and really elevate storytelling

1995
01:36:18.480 --> 01:36:21.393
in a way that nobody else is doing right now,

1996
01:36:23.550 --> 01:36:28.050
is there still an inclination to write a book book

1997
01:36:28.050 --> 01:36:29.940
or is it just like audio is where it's at,

1998
01:36:29.940 --> 01:36:31.245
this is what I'm doing now?

1999
01:36:31.245 --> 01:36:35.280
No, because I think it works in...

2000
01:36:35.280 --> 01:36:38.250
It's very hard to turn a written work

2001
01:36:38.250 --> 01:36:40.020
into a compelling bit of audio

2002
01:36:40.020 --> 01:36:41.760
if you don't have all the tape and all things.

2003
01:36:41.760 --> 01:36:44.220
But you can go in the odder direction.

2004
01:36:44.220 --> 01:36:47.340
So if you read "Talking to Strangers" for example

2005
01:36:47.340 --> 01:36:49.260
or "Bomber Mafia," but "Talking to Strangers"

2006
01:36:49.260 --> 01:36:53.940
was a book I wrote and from the beginning,

2007
01:36:53.940 --> 01:36:55.800
I had the notion that I was going to create

2008
01:36:55.800 --> 01:36:58.860
one of all kinds of special audio books.

2009
01:36:58.860 --> 01:37:01.050
It was gonna be an audio experience,

2010
01:37:01.050 --> 01:37:02.700
so I was collecting all the tape.

2011
01:37:05.049 --> 01:37:06.060
And the way the book is written

2012
01:37:06.060 --> 01:37:11.060
is very much it's written as if I'm reading it to you,

2013
01:37:12.870 --> 01:37:15.300
it's written in a conversational form.

2014
01:37:15.300 --> 01:37:18.810
And that, I think it totally works as a print book.

2015
01:37:18.810 --> 01:37:19.860
It feels a little different,

2016
01:37:19.860 --> 01:37:22.083
it feels more personal and emotional.

2017
01:37:23.730 --> 01:37:27.780
But if you go in that direction, from audio to print,

2018
01:37:27.780 --> 01:37:31.710
I think that works,

2019
01:37:31.710 --> 01:37:33.870
it's just hard to go in the other direction.

2020
01:37:33.870 --> 01:37:34.890
Well, and it also depends

2021
01:37:34.890 --> 01:37:37.800
on what kind of book it is, doesn't it?

2022
01:37:37.800 --> 01:37:39.690
Well, "Talking to Strangers" was a very emotional book,

2023
01:37:39.690 --> 01:37:43.350
it was a series of these very compelling narratives.

2024
01:37:43.350 --> 01:37:45.840
So yeah, for that kind of book, it works.

2025
01:37:45.840 --> 01:37:48.148
You couldn't do an economics textbook that way.

2026
01:37:48.148 --> 01:37:48.981
Right, right, right.

2027
01:37:48.981 --> 01:37:52.830
But it is interesting how entrenched legacy media is

2028
01:37:52.830 --> 01:37:55.070
in the way that they've always done things.

2029
01:37:55.070 --> 01:37:59.100
I know when I wrote my first book in 2012,

2030
01:37:59.100 --> 01:38:01.620
it wasn't even clear that there would be an audio book,

2031
01:38:01.620 --> 01:38:03.090
I almost had to talk them into it.

2032
01:38:03.090 --> 01:38:07.620
And then I did it, and the audio outsells the print,

2033
01:38:07.620 --> 01:38:09.240
and yet they still haven't really...

2034
01:38:09.240 --> 01:38:10.740
Now they take it more seriously

2035
01:38:10.740 --> 01:38:12.780
when you're negotiating your your deal.

2036
01:38:12.780 --> 01:38:14.460
They understand the value of the audio book,

2037
01:38:14.460 --> 01:38:19.460
when not that long ago, it was this cast away afterthought.

2038
01:38:20.130 --> 01:38:23.760
But still, they don't wanna put the time or the resources

2039
01:38:23.760 --> 01:38:27.570
into creating something of quality.

2040
01:38:27.570 --> 01:38:28.403
It's just go in a booth and make your thing.

2041
01:38:28.403 --> 01:38:30.543
Drive me crazy, drives me crazy.

2042
01:38:31.620 --> 01:38:34.440
You'd think they would like, oh this is the future,

2043
01:38:34.440 --> 01:38:38.430
we need to figure out how to be exceptional at this.

2044
01:38:38.430 --> 01:38:41.310
But usually sometimes they do it in-house

2045
01:38:41.310 --> 01:38:43.290
or they just find some third party company

2046
01:38:43.290 --> 01:38:44.953
to do it for them.

2047
01:38:44.953 --> 01:38:47.820
Rich, I hope you call Pushkin Industries

2048
01:38:47.820 --> 01:38:49.868
next time you have an audio book you wanna do.

2049
01:38:49.868 --> 01:38:51.103
I will definitely do that.

2050
01:38:51.103 --> 01:38:54.393
'Cause the more audio you do, you realize like, oh,

2051
01:38:55.680 --> 01:38:59.280
there's so much room for creativity here that is untapped.

2052
01:38:59.280 --> 01:39:01.770
And clearly Pushkin and your team,

2053
01:39:01.770 --> 01:39:05.070
you've realized this, you're an early mover in this space.

2054
01:39:05.070 --> 01:39:09.540
But I think you've only just tapped the tip of the iceberg

2055
01:39:09.540 --> 01:39:11.790
of what's possible.

2056
01:39:11.790 --> 01:39:14.670
The Paul Simon book we did, is a good example of this,

2057
01:39:14.670 --> 01:39:18.180
where it wouldn't work at all as a print book.

2058
01:39:18.180 --> 01:39:20.340
It was so far in the audio direction

2059
01:39:20.340 --> 01:39:24.367
where we just simply I called up Paul Simon and I said,

2060
01:39:24.367 --> 01:39:25.503
"I have an idea.

2061
01:39:27.870 --> 01:39:31.230
Me and my friend Bruce, let us come to you

2062
01:39:31.230 --> 01:39:34.170
and sit down and just have conversations

2063
01:39:34.170 --> 01:39:35.340
and just see where it goes."

2064
01:39:35.340 --> 01:39:37.083
And he said yes to my surprise.

2065
01:39:38.040 --> 01:39:41.640
So we went 10 times, we met with Paul Simon

2066
01:39:41.640 --> 01:39:45.030
in his various places, wherever he was in the world.

2067
01:39:45.030 --> 01:39:48.180
And we would encourage him to have a guitar in his hands

2068
01:39:48.180 --> 01:39:49.830
and we would record it,

2069
01:39:49.830 --> 01:39:51.830
and we were just like, see what happens.

2070
01:39:53.405 --> 01:39:56.880
We had 10, 5-hour conversations.

2071
01:39:56.880 --> 01:40:01.880
And we turned it into a six hour audio book

2072
01:40:02.220 --> 01:40:03.240
called "Miracle and Wonder."

2073
01:40:03.240 --> 01:40:08.240
And it is unlike any other audio book I've ever listened to,

2074
01:40:10.710 --> 01:40:15.600
I won't be so immodest to say it's better, it's different.

2075
01:40:15.600 --> 01:40:18.870
It's like it's not really a book, it's edited conversations

2076
01:40:18.870 --> 01:40:23.870
and then I wrote riffs on trying to make sense of...

2077
01:40:26.803 --> 01:40:27.750
The first chapter is,

2078
01:40:27.750 --> 01:40:31.260
what does it mean that he grew up in Queens in the '50s?

2079
01:40:31.260 --> 01:40:33.570
Does that shape the kind of music that he ends up making?

2080
01:40:33.570 --> 01:40:34.530
I think it does.

2081
01:40:34.530 --> 01:40:36.550
So we talked to him and then I give

2082
01:40:37.590 --> 01:40:42.590
or what's the significance, in retrospect, of Grace Land?

2083
01:40:46.920 --> 01:40:51.360
White guy goes to South Africa in early 1980s

2084
01:40:51.360 --> 01:40:55.620
and wants to sit down with south African musicians

2085
01:40:55.620 --> 01:40:57.750
and create something he doesn't know,

2086
01:40:57.750 --> 01:41:00.150
he can't tell you what it is 'cause he hasn't figured any.

2087
01:41:00.150 --> 01:41:02.190
He just thinks he would be fun to sit down.

2088
01:41:02.190 --> 01:41:04.950
Now, that has political implications, social implications,

2089
01:41:04.950 --> 01:41:07.680
it's the middle of apartheid, creative implications.

2090
01:41:07.680 --> 01:41:11.073
He produces one of the greatest albums of the 20th century.

2091
01:41:12.000 --> 01:41:13.923
How do we think about that in 2022?

2092
01:41:16.440 --> 01:41:19.200
So there's all this really cool things that come up

2093
01:41:19.200 --> 01:41:21.060
when you're talking to someone like that.

2094
01:41:21.060 --> 01:41:24.600
He's been musically relevant five decades,

2095
01:41:24.600 --> 01:41:25.980
how's that possible?

2096
01:41:25.980 --> 01:41:28.710
The thesis being that he's perhaps

2097
01:41:28.710 --> 01:41:33.710
the greatest rockstar of our time because of his ability

2098
01:41:34.290 --> 01:41:37.410
to maintain relevance over so many decades.

2099
01:41:37.410 --> 01:41:40.440
So this is a little hobby horse of mine, Rich.

2100
01:41:40.440 --> 01:41:41.273
When it comes to-

2101
01:41:41.273 --> 01:41:42.810
I know where this is going, go ahead (laughs).

2102
01:41:42.810 --> 01:41:44.400
When it comes to evaluating,

2103
01:41:44.400 --> 01:41:48.480
it's particularly true of athletes and artists,

2104
01:41:48.480 --> 01:41:49.920
when it comes to evaluating

2105
01:41:49.920 --> 01:41:53.400
the greatness of elite performers,

2106
01:41:53.400 --> 01:41:57.030
we overemphasize peak performance

2107
01:41:57.030 --> 01:42:01.080
and underemphasize longevity.

2108
01:42:01.080 --> 01:42:06.080
So I sometimes participate in the LetsRun running website.

2109
01:42:07.530 --> 01:42:11.460
Well, this came up in the extended blog post interview

2110
01:42:11.460 --> 01:42:13.050
that you did with David Epstein

2111
01:42:13.050 --> 01:42:17.190
and then LetsRun erupted in outrage over your thesis.

2112
01:42:17.190 --> 01:42:19.410
They are so wrong and I am so right.

2113
01:42:19.410 --> 01:42:23.167
So I said that Myer Nick Willis,

2114
01:42:23.167 --> 01:42:24.780
"Does not belong in the same conversation

2115
01:42:24.780 --> 01:42:25.613
as Matt Centrowitz."

2116
01:42:25.613 --> 01:42:29.460
And it was Willis is up here, Centrowitz is a step below

2117
01:42:29.460 --> 01:42:31.740
even though LetsRun said Matt Centrowitz

2118
01:42:31.740 --> 01:42:34.590
won the gold medal in the 1500 meters.

2119
01:42:34.590 --> 01:42:36.480
How can you say that Nick Willis,

2120
01:42:36.480 --> 01:42:40.350
who has never won a gold medal in any games is his equal?

2121
01:42:40.350 --> 01:42:42.990
And I would say well, the answer is

2122
01:42:42.990 --> 01:42:47.850
that Matt Centrowitz had one great shining moment,

2123
01:42:47.850 --> 01:42:48.720
that gold medal.

2124
01:42:48.720 --> 01:42:52.290
And he was a relevant runner for three-four year window

2125
01:42:52.290 --> 01:42:55.830
and had one very, very fast 1500 meters that he ran.

2126
01:42:55.830 --> 01:42:58.023
Nick Willis was relevant for 15 years.

2127
01:43:01.521 --> 01:43:03.990
He doesn't have a gold, but he had a silver in a bronze

2128
01:43:03.990 --> 01:43:07.500
in two separate Olympics separated by a lot of years.

2129
01:43:07.500 --> 01:43:10.563
He was a threat to win almost any race he entered.

2130
01:43:11.670 --> 01:43:14.340
For over a decade, his top three times

2131
01:43:14.340 --> 01:43:16.620
were all faster than Matt Centrowitz's times,

2132
01:43:16.620 --> 01:43:19.230
he's broken four minutes for the mile

2133
01:43:19.230 --> 01:43:22.140
in 20 consecutive years.

2134
01:43:22.140 --> 01:43:24.660
And I think as a culture,

2135
01:43:24.660 --> 01:43:26.040
we are somehow dismissive

2136
01:43:26.040 --> 01:43:28.920
of long periods of elite performance

2137
01:43:28.920 --> 01:43:31.800
and infatuated with brief windows

2138
01:43:31.800 --> 01:43:34.350
of extraordinary elite performance,

2139
01:43:34.350 --> 01:43:36.180
and I think that's wrong.

2140
01:43:36.180 --> 01:43:39.960
Well, of course, being as we're getting older,

2141
01:43:39.960 --> 01:43:41.850
we have a longevity bias.

2142
01:43:41.850 --> 01:43:43.413
David accuses you of being,

2143
01:43:44.730 --> 01:43:46.620
of course you're gonna over index on this

2144
01:43:46.620 --> 01:43:48.510
because we're getting old, right?

2145
01:43:48.510 --> 01:43:49.343
Absolutely, 100%, totally.

2146
01:43:49.343 --> 01:43:52.380
But it goes back to the beginning of our conversation,

2147
01:43:52.380 --> 01:43:54.270
when we were talking about kid sports.

2148
01:43:54.270 --> 01:43:58.170
We over index on the highest performers

2149
01:43:58.170 --> 01:44:00.120
and we're not paying attention

2150
01:44:00.120 --> 01:44:04.230
to how we inject these experiences with joy

2151
01:44:04.230 --> 01:44:07.023
to create lifelong pursuits.

2152
01:44:09.120 --> 01:44:11.670
You're right, this is exactly the same conversation.

2153
01:44:11.670 --> 01:44:16.670
Imagine that, Rich, I make you the athletic head

2154
01:44:17.850 --> 01:44:19.890
at one of these local high schools.

2155
01:44:19.890 --> 01:44:22.080
And you stand up in front of the entire school

2156
01:44:22.080 --> 01:44:24.980
on your first day in the job, what is the speech you give?

2157
01:44:25.890 --> 01:44:27.360
Now, I'm gonna put words,

2158
01:44:27.360 --> 01:44:28.193
I know the speech you're gonna give.

2159
01:44:28.193 --> 01:44:29.280
You're gonna write the speech for me.

2160
01:44:29.280 --> 01:44:30.930
Well, I know what you're gonna say.

2161
01:44:30.930 --> 01:44:35.930
You're gonna say, "I am here to create in you, teenagers,

2162
01:44:36.030 --> 01:44:39.330
a set of habits around physical exercise

2163
01:44:39.330 --> 01:44:41.400
that will stay with you for the rest of your life."

2164
01:44:41.400 --> 01:44:44.467
Right?
Mmm-hmm.

2165
01:44:44.467 --> 01:44:47.190
I'm gonna use my job as athletic director

2166
01:44:47.190 --> 01:44:51.180
to teach you how to live a healthy life.

2167
01:44:51.180 --> 01:44:52.170
And you're talking about healthy

2168
01:44:52.170 --> 01:44:53.430
in a sense of physical exercise,

2169
01:44:53.430 --> 01:44:54.600
but all those things as well.

2170
01:44:54.600 --> 01:44:57.570
'Cause that leads you into psychological health,

2171
01:44:57.570 --> 01:44:59.760
emotional health, that's your goal.

2172
01:44:59.760 --> 01:45:01.507
You are not gonna say,

2173
01:45:01.507 --> 01:45:04.650
"I'm gonna try and win as many California State titles

2174
01:45:04.650 --> 01:45:06.690
over the next five years as I possibly can."

2175
01:45:06.690 --> 01:45:08.679
You're not gonna give that.

2176
01:45:08.679 --> 01:45:12.720
And the tragedy is that everybody else who gets that job

2177
01:45:12.720 --> 01:45:14.790
says, "I'm here to win as many California titles

2178
01:45:14.790 --> 01:45:16.890
as I can over the next five years."

2179
01:45:16.890 --> 01:45:18.940
Everyone should give the speech you give,

2180
01:45:20.130 --> 01:45:22.020
that is the speech that's not being given.

2181
01:45:22.020 --> 01:45:26.610
And we are suffering as a country, as a society as a result.

2182
01:45:26.610 --> 01:45:30.930
Look around you, 1% of the population in middle age

2183
01:45:30.930 --> 01:45:33.600
is practicing healthy habits, why is that?

2184
01:45:33.600 --> 01:45:35.550
Because no one bothered to teach them

2185
01:45:35.550 --> 01:45:38.403
at the moment when those habits need to be taught.

2186
01:45:39.360 --> 01:45:41.970
So my argument about Nick Willis,

2187
01:45:41.970 --> 01:45:45.310
the idea of having a standard of performance

2188
01:45:46.266 --> 01:45:49.650
that spans 20 years, that matters, why?

2189
01:45:49.650 --> 01:45:53.667
Because when he 70, Nick's gonna be healthy

2190
01:45:53.667 --> 01:45:57.180
and running and competing, and that's what I want.

2191
01:45:57.180 --> 01:45:59.460
I don't care whether he's winning at 70,

2192
01:45:59.460 --> 01:46:00.870
but the idea that he could put together

2193
01:46:00.870 --> 01:46:03.630
a portfolio of running that starts in his teens

2194
01:46:03.630 --> 01:46:07.120
and ends in his 70s is something that makes me

2195
01:46:09.300 --> 01:46:13.920
just proud to be part of the same group of runners as he is.

2196
01:46:13.920 --> 01:46:17.590
Well, the animating force behind longevity is joy, right?

2197
01:46:17.590 --> 01:46:19.110
Sure.
You can't have

2198
01:46:19.110 --> 01:46:22.500
a longstanding career at a high bar

2199
01:46:22.500 --> 01:46:24.240
unless you're enjoying what you're doing.

2200
01:46:24.240 --> 01:46:27.720
So we have seen outstanding, excellent performances

2201
01:46:27.720 --> 01:46:29.610
from people who reach the very peak.

2202
01:46:29.610 --> 01:46:31.830
But then they burn out or they hate what they're doing

2203
01:46:31.830 --> 01:46:32.970
and they walk away from it,

2204
01:46:32.970 --> 01:46:35.640
never to participate it in it again.

2205
01:46:35.640 --> 01:46:40.260
So how do you ignite that level of joy and connection

2206
01:46:40.260 --> 01:46:43.800
to that pursuit that keeps you going?

2207
01:46:43.800 --> 01:46:47.070
So at your age, you're still interested in running

2208
01:46:47.070 --> 01:46:48.420
and training and all of those things,

2209
01:46:48.420 --> 01:46:51.240
and it brings joy and community into your life.

2210
01:46:51.240 --> 01:46:54.270
And if there's a great performance every once in a while

2211
01:46:54.270 --> 01:46:55.860
that comes out of that, great.

2212
01:46:55.860 --> 01:46:57.483
If not, who cares?

2213
01:46:58.560 --> 01:47:02.400
So I like that argument, it's very Gladwellian of course,

2214
01:47:02.400 --> 01:47:05.733
it's orthogonal to the way that we think about these things.

2215
01:47:06.630 --> 01:47:11.630
But you can't be intellectually honest about this

2216
01:47:11.910 --> 01:47:15.780
unless you recognize that that occasional

2217
01:47:15.780 --> 01:47:20.780
outstanding performer is the spark that inspires us all.

2218
01:47:22.770 --> 01:47:26.250
You need the Michael Phelps's and the LeBrons

2219
01:47:26.250 --> 01:47:30.330
to set this bar that gets us excited

2220
01:47:30.330 --> 01:47:34.200
about maybe engaging in that sport to begin with.

2221
01:47:34.200 --> 01:47:35.033
Yes

2222
01:47:36.330 --> 01:47:38.703
I'm not saying that I'm opposed to-

2223
01:47:40.050 --> 01:47:40.883
Look at how much

2224
01:47:40.883 --> 01:47:43.410
you talk about these fantastic track and field athletes.

2225
01:47:43.410 --> 01:47:45.120
Obviously, these are important to you.

2226
01:47:45.120 --> 01:47:45.953
They are.

2227
01:47:45.953 --> 01:47:50.580
But I also recognize that that set of values

2228
01:47:50.580 --> 01:47:55.580
applied to the body of the sport are counterproductive.

2229
01:47:55.740 --> 01:47:58.896
So what I'm arguing is, it goes back to the conversation

2230
01:47:58.896 --> 01:47:59.729
we had in the very beginning

2231
01:47:59.729 --> 01:48:02.730
about cross country teams should have 20 people on them,

2232
01:48:02.730 --> 01:48:05.670
and the 20th person should matter as much as the first.

2233
01:48:05.670 --> 01:48:06.660
That is not a...

2234
01:48:06.660 --> 01:48:07.710
I'm not saying we run

2235
01:48:07.710 --> 01:48:10.500
the world cross country championships that way.

2236
01:48:10.500 --> 01:48:12.330
But I do think we run the middle school

2237
01:48:12.330 --> 01:48:14.520
and the high school cross country championships that way.

2238
01:48:14.520 --> 01:48:17.160
In other words, it's time for us to understand

2239
01:48:17.160 --> 01:48:20.910
that there are two very different models here.

2240
01:48:20.910 --> 01:48:25.650
And that the models can be contradictory,

2241
01:48:25.650 --> 01:48:29.340
and we need to find a way foster them both.

2242
01:48:29.340 --> 01:48:31.890
And right now, the elite model,

2243
01:48:31.890 --> 01:48:34.110
the peak performance model is winning.

2244
01:48:34.110 --> 01:48:38.383
And that is driving a lot of young people out of the sport

2245
01:48:38.383 --> 01:48:42.420
and discouraging a lot of people and frustrating our attempt

2246
01:48:42.420 --> 01:48:46.050
to restore the health of our society.

2247
01:48:46.050 --> 01:48:48.750
And on that subject of health and and mental health,

2248
01:48:48.750 --> 01:48:49.890
as we touched on earlier,

2249
01:48:49.890 --> 01:48:52.800
did you see the story about Terry McKeever,

2250
01:48:52.800 --> 01:48:56.010
the head women's swimming coach at Berkeley?

2251
01:48:56.010 --> 01:48:56.843
No.

2252
01:48:56.843 --> 01:49:01.650
So she's considered the most legendary coach,

2253
01:49:01.650 --> 01:49:03.870
produced Olympians, world record blah, blah, blah

2254
01:49:03.870 --> 01:49:05.670
for decade after decade after decade.

2255
01:49:05.670 --> 01:49:07.500
And now coming out of the woodwork

2256
01:49:07.500 --> 01:49:09.850
are all of these former athletes of hers

2257
01:49:10.950 --> 01:49:14.760
telling tales of abuse and eating disorders

2258
01:49:14.760 --> 01:49:17.100
and suicides and the like

2259
01:49:17.100 --> 01:49:21.840
because that reign was marked by terror.

2260
01:49:21.840 --> 01:49:24.760
These young athletes being so terrified of their coach

2261
01:49:26.310 --> 01:49:29.880
and that coach holding them to such a high standard

2262
01:49:29.880 --> 01:49:31.860
that yes, it produces champions

2263
01:49:31.860 --> 01:49:34.260
but it produces a lot of carnage along the way.

2264
01:49:34.260 --> 01:49:37.440
And I feel like socially as a culture,

2265
01:49:37.440 --> 01:49:40.080
we're reckoning with that kind of legacy

2266
01:49:40.080 --> 01:49:42.450
modality of coaching in a healthy way.

2267
01:49:42.450 --> 01:49:45.630
But we're still purging ourselves

2268
01:49:45.630 --> 01:49:49.740
of the ills of that philosophy of sport.

2269
01:49:49.740 --> 01:49:51.130
Yeah, we have a long way to go in that.

2270
01:49:51.130 --> 01:49:52.173
Yeah, I know.

2271
01:49:53.040 --> 01:49:54.060
Well, before I let you go,

2272
01:49:54.060 --> 01:49:57.150
the final thing I can't let you go without asking you about

2273
01:49:57.150 --> 01:50:02.150
is so much of your work is about, as you just illustrated,

2274
01:50:02.310 --> 01:50:03.930
looking at things through a different lens

2275
01:50:03.930 --> 01:50:08.930
or deconstructing conventional wisdom.

2276
01:50:09.060 --> 01:50:11.580
What else, it doesn't have to be sports related,

2277
01:50:11.580 --> 01:50:14.190
is making you nuts right now?

2278
01:50:14.190 --> 01:50:16.170
Or what would you change

2279
01:50:16.170 --> 01:50:18.933
that you see around you in your daily life?

2280
01:50:20.820 --> 01:50:23.070
Oh, wow, the list of things that I'd like to change-

2281
01:50:23.070 --> 01:50:24.180
There's probably a lot, right?

2282
01:50:24.180 --> 01:50:27.360
In the world is so long.

2283
01:50:27.360 --> 01:50:29.520
And I didn't even get to education

2284
01:50:29.520 --> 01:50:31.920
That was gonna be whole hour of this podcast,

2285
01:50:31.920 --> 01:50:32.870
so maybe another time.

2286
01:50:32.870 --> 01:50:33.990
In the beginning.

2287
01:50:33.990 --> 01:50:38.100
I guess I would only say

2288
01:50:38.100 --> 01:50:40.020
I would follow up on what we've been talking about.

2289
01:50:40.020 --> 01:50:43.410
Which is, I have been thinking really long and hard

2290
01:50:43.410 --> 01:50:48.410
about competition and when it's useful and when it's not.

2291
01:50:55.860 --> 01:50:57.870
I'm someone who is deeply competitive

2292
01:50:57.870 --> 01:51:02.640
and who growing up there was not a game or a sport

2293
01:51:02.640 --> 01:51:04.980
or a thing that I didn't try to win at,

2294
01:51:04.980 --> 01:51:08.010
you know I'm that kind of person.

2295
01:51:08.010 --> 01:51:09.840
And now I'm beginning to understand

2296
01:51:09.840 --> 01:51:11.640
that there are lots of places

2297
01:51:11.640 --> 01:51:14.970
where that attitude is productive.

2298
01:51:14.970 --> 01:51:19.970
And not just in sports, but across all...

2299
01:51:20.040 --> 01:51:23.040
Like I was chatting with my brother, I saw him this weekend,

2300
01:51:23.040 --> 01:51:25.473
who's a retired elementary school principal.

2301
01:51:26.520 --> 01:51:27.930
I was talking about my obsession

2302
01:51:27.930 --> 01:51:29.580
with all this relative age effect stuff.

2303
01:51:29.580 --> 01:51:32.970
And he goes "Well," we were talking about possible solutions

2304
01:51:32.970 --> 01:51:37.800
and he goes, "Well, we don't have to rank and grade kids

2305
01:51:37.800 --> 01:51:40.560
under the age of 14 or 15.

2306
01:51:40.560 --> 01:51:43.320
There's no particular need to, what's at stake?"

2307
01:51:43.320 --> 01:51:46.710
We could start doing it at high school if you like,

2308
01:51:46.710 --> 01:51:48.760
but you could just stop grading them

2309
01:51:49.680 --> 01:51:52.200
in elementary school and middle school.

2310
01:51:52.200 --> 01:51:53.790
You could teach the same things

2311
01:51:53.790 --> 01:51:56.733
and keep tabs on who's behind.

2312
01:52:00.210 --> 01:52:02.040
My brother also, a very competitive guy,

2313
01:52:02.040 --> 01:52:04.440
was a fantastic athlete in this day, he's not...

2314
01:52:04.440 --> 01:52:08.610
But after 40 years in the public school system

2315
01:52:08.610 --> 01:52:10.740
as an elementary school teacher and principal,

2316
01:52:10.740 --> 01:52:12.480
he was like, "There's just no point to it."

2317
01:52:12.480 --> 01:52:14.929
Is this doing more hard than good?

2318
01:52:14.929 --> 01:52:18.000
And that's a really, really useful question

2319
01:52:18.000 --> 01:52:20.133
to start answering in a lot of domains.

2320
01:52:21.180 --> 01:52:23.790
That does bring up the education discussion though.

2321
01:52:23.790 --> 01:52:26.820
So much of education is premised on competition,

2322
01:52:26.820 --> 01:52:28.890
from the way that colleges are ranked,

2323
01:52:28.890 --> 01:52:31.350
and you have a whole thing about that,

2324
01:52:31.350 --> 01:52:36.350
to the whole ecosystem around getting kids into college

2325
01:52:38.160 --> 01:52:40.590
and what that means and what that doesn't mean.

2326
01:52:40.590 --> 01:52:44.130
That is so retrograde in so many ways

2327
01:52:44.130 --> 01:52:45.900
and does so much damage to young people.

2328
01:52:45.900 --> 01:52:50.900
Especially in this era in which

2329
01:52:51.150 --> 01:52:55.830
so much of what higher education is about is really detached

2330
01:52:55.830 --> 01:52:59.913
from what is necessary to be productive in the world.

2331
01:53:03.180 --> 01:53:04.590
I had a conversation last night with a friend

2332
01:53:04.590 --> 01:53:05.610
who was trying to figure out

2333
01:53:05.610 --> 01:53:07.620
where to send her daughter to school.

2334
01:53:07.620 --> 01:53:11.913
And she said, well, she really should go to, I think,

2335
01:53:13.920 --> 01:53:15.390
some college in Boulder.

2336
01:53:15.390 --> 01:53:16.223
UC, Boulder.

2337
01:53:16.223 --> 01:53:17.056
Probably.

2338
01:53:17.056 --> 01:53:19.290
'Cause she's outdoorsy, she likes to hang.

2339
01:53:19.290 --> 01:53:21.060
Well, when I suggested that to my daughter,

2340
01:53:21.060 --> 01:53:22.380
she burst into tears

2341
01:53:22.380 --> 01:53:24.540
'cause her daughter wants to go to a school

2342
01:53:24.540 --> 01:53:27.990
that other people think is a good school for her.

2343
01:53:27.990 --> 01:53:29.580
It's so weirdly detached

2344
01:53:29.580 --> 01:53:33.450
that objectively this is a place where she would be happy.

2345
01:53:33.450 --> 01:53:35.040
And yet she doesn't wanna go there

2346
01:53:35.040 --> 01:53:36.090
because the world tells her

2347
01:53:36.090 --> 01:53:40.560
that you have to go to a place that's ranked one through 25.

2348
01:53:40.560 --> 01:53:42.510
And schools being brands,

2349
01:53:42.510 --> 01:53:47.221
and these brands have a lot of power and resonance

2350
01:53:47.221 --> 01:53:49.623
in a way that is really damaging.

2351
01:53:51.240 --> 01:53:54.060
That's a whole thing, we should maybe do that another time.

2352
01:53:54.060 --> 01:53:57.870
One last thing, I had a conversation with Adam Grant,

2353
01:53:57.870 --> 01:53:59.697
we love to have these arguments every now and again.

2354
01:53:59.697 --> 01:54:01.980
I was thinking about what I would like to do

2355
01:54:01.980 --> 01:54:04.860
is in a very subversive way, screw up the whole system.

2356
01:54:04.860 --> 01:54:07.650
And I said to Adam, "What if you created schools

2357
01:54:07.650 --> 01:54:11.070
that had totally arbitrary admissions criteria?"

2358
01:54:11.070 --> 01:54:14.760
So what if you had a school where you said, all right,

2359
01:54:14.760 --> 01:54:17.460
in order to be considered for admission to our school,

2360
01:54:17.460 --> 01:54:22.110
you've gotta be able to break six minutes in a mile for men

2361
01:54:22.110 --> 01:54:25.830
and for women, 6:30, let's just say.

2362
01:54:25.830 --> 01:54:28.830
Well, let's have the bar a little higher for women.

2363
01:54:28.830 --> 01:54:31.950
Like just random shit like that and see what happens.

2364
01:54:31.950 --> 01:54:36.870
Well, if you got someone like Adam to design the testing,

2365
01:54:36.870 --> 01:54:39.570
you could select for the kind of people

2366
01:54:39.570 --> 01:54:41.430
that would get along with each other

2367
01:54:41.430 --> 01:54:43.500
and benefit from being in each other's company.

2368
01:54:43.500 --> 01:54:45.960
Would you like to go to an academically rigorous school

2369
01:54:45.960 --> 01:54:48.813
that was made up 100% of competitive athletes?

2370
01:54:50.040 --> 01:54:55.040
When I say competitive, people who take athletics seriously.

2371
01:54:55.200 --> 01:54:57.210
I actually think that would be super-interesting.

2372
01:54:57.210 --> 01:54:59.040
I don't know, would I learn something different

2373
01:54:59.040 --> 01:55:00.180
than the random school I went to?

2374
01:55:00.180 --> 01:55:02.463
Yeah, I think so, worth trying.

2375
01:55:03.480 --> 01:55:05.940
Then making that a reality is a whole different thing.

2376
01:55:05.940 --> 01:55:06.773
I know.

2377
01:55:08.010 --> 01:55:11.220
Thank you Malcolm Gladwell, I really appreciate your time.

2378
01:55:11.220 --> 01:55:12.780
Super-fun to talk to you

2379
01:55:12.780 --> 01:55:13.613
Thank you, Rich, thanks for having me.

2380
01:55:13.613 --> 01:55:15.007
That's fantastic.

2381
01:55:15.007 --> 01:55:17.790
"Legacy of Speed," Pushkin Industries,

2382
01:55:17.790 --> 01:55:21.090
you can find it wherever you listen to or find podcasts.

2383
01:55:21.090 --> 01:55:24.060
And Malcolm's pretty easy to find on the internet,

2384
01:55:24.060 --> 01:55:25.380
I don't think anyone's gonna have any trouble

2385
01:55:25.380 --> 01:55:26.670
tracking you down.

2386
01:55:26.670 --> 01:55:27.963
You got a newsletter,

2387
01:55:29.190 --> 01:55:31.087
what else do you wanna draw people' attention to?

2388
01:55:31.087 --> 01:55:33.030
"Revisionist History."

2389
01:55:33.030 --> 01:55:34.150
Season seven?

2390
01:55:34.150 --> 01:55:37.530
Seven drops this coming Friday, June 30th.

2391
01:55:37.530 --> 01:55:39.480
And it's all about experiments, right?

2392
01:55:39.480 --> 01:55:42.240
It's not out as of the moment we're recording this,

2393
01:55:42.240 --> 01:55:43.290
but I'm looking forward to that.

2394
01:55:43.290 --> 01:55:46.290
Anything that involves Australian swimmers, I'm in.

2395
01:55:46.290 --> 01:55:47.123
Yeah, it's all there.

2396
01:55:47.123 --> 01:55:47.956
All right, cool.

2397
01:55:47.956 --> 01:55:49.950
Well, come and talk to me again sometime I hope.

2398
01:55:49.950 --> 01:55:51.090
Thank you, thank you.

2399
01:55:51.090 --> 01:55:52.189
All right, peace.

2400
01:55:52.189 --> 01:55:54.856
(wistful music)

